export const TOPIC_FACTS: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "Guide dogs": [
    "Britain's first four guide dogs were German Shepherds called Flash, Judy, Meta and Folly. On 6 October 1931, in Wallasey on Merseyside, they were handed over to their new owners, men who had lost their sight fighting in the First World War.",
    "Britain's guide dogs began in a lock-up garage. In 1931 two women, Muriel Crooke and Rosamund Bond, organised the training of the first four British guide dogs from a garage in Wallasey, Merseyside, with help from a trainer sent by Dorothy Eustis, who ran guide dog schools in Switzerland and America.",
    "The Guide Dogs for the Blind Association was founded in 1934, three years after Britain's first guide dogs qualified. Its first permanent trainer was a Russian army officer, Captain Nikolai Liakhoff, who had moved to Britain in 1933.",
    "The world's first guide dog school opened in Germany in August 1916, during the First World War. It was started by Dr Gerhard Stalling to train dogs for soldiers who had been blinded in the fighting.",
    "America's first famous guide dog was a German Shepherd called Buddy. A young blind man, Morris Frank, travelled to Switzerland in 1928 to train with her. She was really called Kiss, but he renamed her Buddy, and all six of his guide dogs after her were called Buddy too.",
    "The Seeing Eye, the famous American guide dog school, was set up in 1929 by Dorothy Eustis and Morris Frank. Its name comes from a line in the Bible, the hearing ear and the seeing eye.",
    "Today Guide Dogs, the British charity, is the world's largest breeder and trainer of working dogs. Since 1931, it says, more than 36,000 lives have been changed by a guide dog partnership.",
    "A guide dog does not know where its owner wants to go. The person plans the route and gives directions such as forward, left and right, and the dog's job is to steer them safely round obstacles, stop at kerbs and find doors, steps and crossings.",
    "Guide dogs cannot tell a red traffic light from a green one. At a road, the dog stops at the kerb, and its owner listens to the traffic and decides when it is safe to cross.",
    "A guide dog wears a harness with a stiff handle. Through the handle, its owner can feel every turn, stop and step the dog makes, so the two move together almost as one.",
    "Before training, a guide dog puppy spends about its first year living with a volunteer family, called puppy raisers. They teach it good manners and take it on buses, into shops and through busy streets, so nothing in the world outside frightens it.",
    "Most guide dogs in Britain today are Labradors, Golden Retrievers, or a cross of the two. Britain's very first guide dogs were German Shepherds.",
    "Under British law, shops, restaurants, taxis and other businesses must not turn away a person because they have a guide dog or another trained assistance dog. The Equality Act 2010 protects their right to go where everyone else can.",
    "You should never stroke, feed or call a guide dog while it is wearing its harness. It is working, and distracting it could put its owner in danger. If you would like to say hello, ask the owner first.",
    "Guide dogs usually retire at around ten years old. Some stay with their owner as a pet when a new guide dog arrives, and others go to live with the family who raised them as a puppy or with a new home.",
    "Guide dogs are even trained to go to the toilet on a word of command, in a sensible place, so that their owner knows when and where it has happened.",
    "Puppies in the same guide dog litter are often given names starting with the same letter, which helps the charity keep track of who belongs to which family.",
    "A wall painting found in the ruins of Herculaneum, the Roman town buried by Mount Vesuvius in AD 79, is thought to show a blind man being led by a dog, which suggests guide dogs are nearly 2,000 years old.",
    "A statue of a guide dog stands in Wallasey, Merseyside, outside the Floral Pavilion theatre, to mark the town where Britain's first guide dogs were trained in 1931.",
    "The idea for The Seeing Eye began with a magazine article. In 1927 the American dog trainer Dorothy Eustis wrote about the guide dogs she had seen in Germany, and a blind reader, Morris Frank, wrote to her asking for a dog of his own.",
  ],
  "Medical detection dogs": [
    "The charity Medical Detection Dogs was co-founded in 2008 by Dr Claire Guest. A year later her own Labrador, Daisy, alerted her to an early breast cancer. The charity is based at Great Horwood, near Milton Keynes.",
    "Medical detection dogs do two different jobs. Medical alert dogs live with one person and warn them when their body starts to go wrong. Bio-detection dogs work in a laboratory, sniffing samples to help scientists learn what diseases smell like.",
    "In a study published in 2025, two dogs, a Golden Retriever called Bumper and a Black Labrador called Peanut, learned to pick out skin swabs from people with Parkinson's disease. They found most of the Parkinson's samples and very rarely made a false alarm.",
    "Bio-detection dogs often work at a scent carousel, a row of stands each holding a sample. The dog walks along sniffing each one, and sits or freezes beside the sample that smells of the disease it has been trained to find, earning a treat.",
    "Illness can change a person's smell. Our bodies give off tiny amounts of chemicals called volatile organic compounds in our breath, sweat and skin, and some diseases change the mix. Dogs can smell these changes when people cannot.",
    "A dog's nose has up to 300 million scent receptors, compared with about 6 million in a human's. The part of a dog's brain that makes sense of smells is also much bigger, for its size, than ours.",
    "In 2020 and 2021, dogs trained by Medical Detection Dogs, with scientists from the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine and Durham University, showed they could sniff out the smell of COVID-19 in samples from people.",
    "In a 2018 study, dogs were trained to sniff socks worn by children in The Gambia, in West Africa, and could pick out many of the children who had malaria, even though they had no signs of being ill.",
    "In the Netherlands, a Beagle called Cliff was trained to sniff out a dangerous stomach bug, Clostridium difficile, in hospital patients. His success was reported in the British Medical Journal in 2012.",
    "Some dogs are trained to warn people with epilepsy before a seizure. In Britain, the charity Support Dogs in Sheffield trains seizure alert dogs, which give their owner time to get somewhere safe.",
    "Diabetes alert dogs can smell when their owner's blood sugar is dropping too low or rising too high, often before the person notices. They alert by nudging, pawing or staring, and some even fetch the owner's testing kit.",
    "Some medical alert dogs help people with severe allergies. They are trained to sniff out tiny traces of the food, such as nuts, that could make their owner dangerously ill.",
    "Medical alert dogs also help people with conditions such as Addison's disease and PoTS, which can make someone suddenly faint or become very unwell. The dog learns its owner's normal smell and warns them when something changes.",
    "Myth: A dog can diagnose your cancer. The truth: Dogs have shown in published studies that they can detect the smell of some diseases, but they are not used by doctors to diagnose anyone. Their biggest job may be helping scientists discover what disease smells like, so machines can one day do it.",
    "Trials with dogs have been run with NHS hospitals, including Milton Keynes University Hospital, looking at prostate cancer, and Hull and East Yorkshire Hospitals, looking at bowel cancer.",
    "According to Medical Detection Dogs, dogs can smell some chemicals at concentrations of parts per trillion. That is like being able to notice a single teaspoon of sugar stirred into two Olympic-sized swimming pools of water.",
    "To a medical detection dog, the work is a game. It does not know what cancer or Parkinson's disease is. It only knows that when it finds the special smell, it gets a reward, which is usually a treat or a favourite toy.",
    "Medical alert dogs are assistance dogs, so in Britain they can go into shops, restaurants and on public transport with their owner. They wear a jacket to show they are working, and should not be distracted.",
    "Training a bio-detection dog takes months of practice with many samples, because the dog must learn to ignore every other smell and pick out only the one it is looking for, however faint.",
    "Scientists are trying to build electronic noses that copy what dogs can do. Dogs help by showing which samples really do smell different, so the machine's makers know what to look for.",
  ],
  "Search and rescue dogs": [
    "Britain's mountain search dogs began with one man, Hamish MacInnes, leader of the Glencoe Mountain Rescue Team. In the early 1960s he went to Switzerland to see avalanche rescue dogs at work, and realised dogs could find walkers lost on British hills.",
    "Hamish MacInnes could not take his own dogs to the Swiss avalanche course because of Britain's strict quarantine rules. He learned all he could, came home and trained his two German Shepherds, Rangi and Tiki.",
    "The first national training course for British mountain rescue dogs was held in Glencoe in December 1964. Handlers came from Scotland, England and Wales, and the BBC came to film it.",
    "The Search and Rescue Dog Association, known as SARDA, was formed in May 1965 at a meeting in Hamish MacInnes's house in Glencoe. By 1971 it had split into Scottish, English and Welsh associations.",
    "Early British search dogs were graded by letter: A for a novice, B for a more experienced dog, and C for a dog that had actually found someone on a real call-out.",
    "Hamish MacInnes, who started Britain's mountain search dogs, was also an inventor. He designed the first all-metal ice axe and a lightweight mountain rescue stretcher. He died in Glencoe in 2020, aged 90.",
    "Today there are twelve recognised search and rescue dog organisations in the UK and Ireland, covering mountains, lowlands and water, all growing from the first association started in Glencoe in 1965.",
    "Some search dogs are trailing dogs. They are given something the missing person has worn or touched, then follow that one person's scent, ignoring everyone else who has walked the same way.",
    "Avalanche dogs can find people buried under snow. Human scent rises up through the snow, and a trained dog can pinpoint where it is strongest and start digging.",
    "When a search dog finds someone, it has to tell its handler. Some dogs bark and stay beside the person, while others run back to the handler and lead them to the spot.",
    "Search dogs are trained with the help of volunteers who hide on hillsides, in woods or in ruined buildings, waiting to be found. For the dog it is the best game of hide and seek, with a reward at the end.",
    "A search dog working at night usually wears a light, and sometimes a bell, on its collar, so its handler can follow where it goes in the dark.",
    "One search dog can cover a big stretch of hillside far faster than a line of people, because it does not need to see the missing person. It follows the scent on the air.",
    "During the Blitz in London, a stray dog called Rip was taken in by an air raid warden in Poplar. With no training at all, he began sniffing out people buried under bombed buildings, and he won the Dickin Medal in 1945.",
    "Beauty, a Wire Fox Terrier who belonged to a PDSA rescue worker, dug out animals trapped under bombed buildings during the Second World War. She is said to have found 63 animals, and won the Dickin Medal in 1945.",
    "Some British fire and rescue services have specially trained search dogs for collapsed buildings. UK rescue teams have taken search dogs abroad to help after earthquakes, such as the one in Turkey in 2023.",
    "Water search dogs can work from a boat. Scent can rise up from under the water, and a trained dog leans out and signals when it catches it, helping divers know where to look.",
    "The dogs used for search and rescue in Britain are often Border Collies, Labradors, spaniels and German Shepherds: breeds with good noses, lots of stamina and a love of working with people.",
    "Mountain and lowland search dogs in Britain are trained to ignore sheep, deer and other animals on the hills, so they keep their noses on the job of finding people.",
    "Most search and rescue dogs in Britain live at home as family pets with their handlers, who are volunteers. When a call-out comes, dog and handler set off together, often in the middle of the night.",
  ],
  "Emotional support and therapy dogs": [
    "An emotional support dog is a pet that comforts its owner simply by being there. Unlike a guide dog or other assistance dog, it is not trained to do special tasks.",
    "In Britain, emotional support dogs have no special legal status. Unlike assistance dogs, they do not have a legal right to go into shops, restaurants or other places where pets are not allowed.",
    "Therapy dogs visit hospitals, care homes, hospices, schools and libraries with their owners to cheer people up. They bring comfort, but they do not have the legal rights of assistance dogs.",
    "The charity Pets As Therapy was founded in Britain in 1983. Its volunteers take their calm, friendly dogs to visit people in hospitals, care homes and schools, and it has worked with more than 20,000 dogs.",
    "Stroking a friendly dog can make your body release oxytocin, a hormone linked to feeling calm and close to others. It may also lower stress.",
    "In 2015, scientists in Japan found that when dogs and their owners gazed into each other's eyes, levels of oxytocin, the bonding hormone, rose in both of them.",
    "Some schools and libraries run reading-to-dogs schemes, where children read aloud to a calm dog. The dog does not mind mistakes, which can help nervous readers grow more confident.",
    "In 2016, scientists from the University of Lincoln showed that dogs can recognise human emotions. Dogs matched happy or angry faces with happy or angry voices, something once thought only people could do.",
    "Mental health assistance dogs are different from emotional support dogs. They are properly trained to help with a person's condition, for example by leaning against them to calm them, or making space around them in a crowd, and in Britain they do have legal rights of access.",
    "Hearing Dogs for Deaf People was founded in Britain in 1982. Its dogs alert deaf owners to sounds such as the doorbell, the oven timer and the smoke alarm, and give them company too. They wear burgundy coats.",
    "Many universities invite therapy dogs onto campus at exam time, so stressed students can spend a few minutes stroking a calm dog before getting back to revision.",
    "Any breed can be an emotional support dog. What matters is a gentle, calm nature and a love of people, rather than size or looks.",
    "In the United States, emotional support animals used to be allowed to fly in aircraft cabins, but from 2021 the rules changed so that airlines only have to accept trained service dogs.",
    "Before a dog can become a Pets As Therapy dog, it is assessed to check it is calm and gentle, stays relaxed when strangers stroke it, and does not mind sudden noises.",
    "Visits from therapy dogs can help people living with dementia in care homes. Someone who rarely talks may start chatting while stroking a dog, or remember a dog they once had.",
    "Walking a dog helps people meet each other. Dog owners often stop and chat to other dog walkers, which can make people feel less lonely.",
    "Some children's hospital wards welcome visits from therapy dogs, which can take a child's mind off being in hospital, even for a few minutes.",
    "Dogs seem to notice when people are upset. Many owners say their dog comes to sit close or rest its head on them when they are sad, and scientists are studying how dogs read our feelings.",
    "A therapy dog's visits are short and gentle, because comforting lots of strangers is tiring. Good handlers watch their dog for signs it needs a rest.",
    "Pets As Therapy also runs a reading scheme called Read2Dogs, taking its dogs into schools so that children can practise reading aloud to a friendly, patient listener.",
  ],
  "Dogs in Downing Street": [
    "Winston Churchill's wartime dog was Rufus, a brown Poodle. Rufus was a close companion through the Second World War, and lived with Churchill at 10 Downing Street.",
    "Churchill's Poodle Rufus was not allowed into Cabinet meetings. Churchill is said to have told him that he had not found it necessary to ask him to join the wartime Cabinet.",
    "Rufus, Churchill's Poodle, went with him on his wartime travels, including a voyage across the Atlantic to meet the American President, Franklin Roosevelt.",
    "Churchill's first Poodle, Rufus, was run over and killed in 1947. Churchill was heartbroken, and his next Poodle was named Rufus too, known as Rufus II.",
    "Winston Churchill's first dog was a Bulldog called Dodo. He sold his bicycle to buy it when he was a pupil at Harrow School.",
    "For most of his life, Winston Churchill believed that dogs did not like him. That changed during the Second World War, when he got his Poodle, Rufus.",
    "Clement Attlee, who became Prime Minister in 1945, was often seen outside Number 10 with his Airedale Terrier, called Ting.",
    "Sir Alec Douglas-Home, Prime Minister in 1963 and 1964, had a black Labrador. A 1980 portrait of him fishing, now in the National Portrait Gallery, shows the dog sitting in the corner.",
    "Harold Wilson, Prime Minister in the 1960s and 1970s, had a yellow Labrador called Paddy.",
    "Anthony Eden, Prime Minister from 1955 to 1957, is said to have had a Cairn Terrier for company.",
    "In 2019, Boris Johnson and his partner Carrie brought Dilyn, a Jack Russell cross, to live in Downing Street. They adopted him from the charity Friends of Animals Wales.",
    "Dilyn, Boris Johnson's dog, was a rescue from a puppy farm. His jaw was crooked, so the farm could not sell him, and he might have been put down. Instead he ended up living at 10 Downing Street.",
    "Dilyn's owners used his story to support Lucy's Law, a rule that stops pet shops and dealers in England from selling puppies and kittens they have not bred themselves, to help crack down on puppy farms.",
    "When Dilyn the dog moved into Downing Street in 2019, people wondered whether Larry, the Downing Street cat, would retire. He did not: Larry stayed put.",
    "When Boris Johnson left Downing Street in 2022, his dog Dilyn left with him. Larry the cat stayed behind, as the building's permanent resident, reminding everyone that prime ministers come and go.",
    "Rishi Sunak brought Nova, a fox red Labrador, to Downing Street in 2022. The family got her after their daughters met Boris Johnson's dog, Dilyn.",
    "Nova the Labrador made her first public appearance outside 10 Downing Street in October 2022, when Rishi Sunak and his wife bought poppies and a special poppy dog collar for her from the Royal British Legion.",
    "Nova, Rishi Sunak's Labrador, and Larry, the Downing Street cat, did not always get along. Rishi Sunak's wife, Akshata Murty, said they had had some heated exchanges, and that Larry usually won.",
    "Larry, the Downing Street cat, came from Battersea Dogs and Cats Home, the famous London animal rescue centre, in February 2011. He has lived at Number 10 alongside the dogs of several prime ministers.",
    "Cats have usually outnumbered dogs at Downing Street. One list of prime ministers' pets records no dogs at all for the prime ministers from Edward Heath to Theresa May.",
  ],
  "Military dogs": [
    "The British War Dog School was set up in 1917, during the First World War, by Edwin Richardson, a dog trainer who had campaigned for the army to use dogs. It was based at Shoeburyness in Essex and trained more than 200 dogs.",
    "In the First World War, messenger dogs ran through mud, shellfire and barbed wire carrying messages in tins on their collars. In 1916 two Airedale Terriers, Wolf and Prince, proved so reliable that officers began using dogs for many more jobs.",
    "Bing, an Alsatian and Collie cross from Loughborough, was lent to the army in 1944 because his family could not feed him during wartime rationing. His real name was Brian.",
    "Bing the paradog jumped into Normandy with the 13th Parachute Battalion on D-Day, 6 June 1944, and later parachuted over the River Rhine. He won the Dickin Medal in 1947 and went home to his family after the war.",
    "Army paradogs had their own harness and parachute, which opened automatically. The dogs learned to jump straight out of the aircraft after their handler, expecting a tasty reward when they landed.",
    "Rob was a Collie from a farm in Shropshire who served with the SAS in the Second World War and was said to have made more than 20 parachute jumps. He won the Dickin Medal in 1945, though years later some claimed his jumps were exaggerated.",
    "After D-Day, army dogs worked with engineers to find landmines, including ones made without metal, which mine detectors could not find. Dogs were said to be the quickest way of locating minefields.",
    "Judy, a Pointer, was the ship's dog on the Royal Navy gunboats HMS Gnat and HMS Grasshopper. After her ship sank she was held in a Japanese prison camp, where she kept up the prisoners' spirits. She won the Dickin Medal in 1946.",
    "Theo, an English Springer Spaniel, searched for hidden bombs and weapons with his handler, Lance Corporal Liam Tasker, in Afghanistan. When Liam was killed in 2011, Theo died just hours later. Theo was awarded the Dickin Medal in 2012.",
    "Sasha, a Labrador, searched for explosives with the Parachute Regiment in Afghanistan in 2008 and was awarded the Dickin Medal in 2014 for outstanding bravery.",
    "The Dickin Medal is a bronze medal inscribed For Gallantry and We Also Serve. Its ribbon is striped green, dark brown and pale blue, for water, earth and air, standing for the navy, the army and the air force.",
    "The Dickin Medal was created in 1943 by Maria Dickin, who founded the animal charity PDSA in 1917. The very first medals went to three pigeons.",
    "Kuno, a Belgian Malinois, served with British special forces in Afghanistan and was badly injured in 2019. He was fitted with special artificial paws and awarded the Dickin Medal in 2020.",
    "Britain's military dogs are trained at the Defence Animal Training Regiment at Melton Mowbray in Leicestershire, which also trains the army's horses.",
    "The Animals in War Memorial in Park Lane, London, was unveiled in 2004. It honours all the animals that served and died in wars, with the words: They had no choice.",
    "Military dogs do many jobs: searching for explosives and weapons, guarding bases, patrolling, tracking and protecting their handlers. Each dog works closely with one handler, and the two become a team.",
    "During the Second World War, the army asked British families to lend their pet dogs for war work. Many were accepted, trained and, like Bing the paradog, returned home after the war.",
    "Lucca, a German Shepherd who served with the United States Marines, completed hundreds of missions in Iraq and Afghanistan finding explosives, and was awarded the Dickin Medal in 2016.",
    "Military dogs often retire at around eight to ten years old. Many go to live with their old handler, and others are rehomed with families.",
    "Dogs have gone to war for thousands of years. Ancient armies used big guard dogs in battle, but today military dogs are mostly used for their noses, sniffing out danger to keep soldiers safe.",
  ],
  "Space dogs": [
    "Laika was the first animal ever to orbit the Earth. She was launched by the Soviet Union aboard Sputnik 2 on 3 November 1957.",
    "Laika was a stray dog found on the streets of Moscow. Soviet scientists chose stray dogs for space because they thought dogs used to hunger and cold would cope better with hard conditions.",
    "Myth: Laika lived happily in orbit for about a week. The truth: For many years the Soviet Union claimed this, but in 2002 it was revealed that she died within hours of launch, from overheating. There was never any plan to bring her home.",
    "The name Laika means barker in Russian. She was a small mixed-breed dog, and in the West newspapers nicknamed her Muttnik.",
    "In 2008 a monument to Laika was unveiled in Moscow, near the research centre where she was trained. It shows her standing on top of a rocket.",
    "The first dogs to go into space and come back alive were Dezik and Tsygan. On 22 July 1951 they flew up to the edge of space in a Soviet rocket and returned safely by parachute.",
    "In August 1960 two dogs, Belka and Strelka, orbited the Earth aboard Sputnik 5 and came home safely. They were among the first living things to go into orbit and return alive, along with mice, rats and flies.",
    "One of Strelka's puppies, called Pushinka, was given as a present by the Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev to Caroline Kennedy, daughter of the American President John F. Kennedy, in 1961. Pushinka later had puppies with a Kennedy dog.",
    "The Soviet space dogs were usually small females. Small dogs fitted into the tiny capsules, and the space suits were thought to work better for female dogs.",
    "In 1966 two Soviet dogs, Veterok and Ugolyok, spent 22 days in orbit, a record for dogs in space.",
    "Just weeks before Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space in 1961, a dog called Zvezdochka flew one of the final test flights. Her name means Little Star, and Gagarin is said to have chosen it.",
    "In March 1961, a dog called Chernushka orbited the Earth with a life-size dummy cosmonaut called Ivan Ivanovich, as a test before the first human flight.",
    "Soviet space dogs were trained to sit still in tiny boxes for days, to wear space suits, and to cope with loud noises and the shaking of a rocket launch.",
    "While the Soviet Union sent dogs into space, the United States mainly used monkeys and chimpanzees for its early test flights.",
    "Astro, the Great Dane in the 1960s cartoon The Jetsons, lives in a future world of flying cars and robot helpers. He is famous for saying Ruh-roh.",
    "In A Grand Day Out, the first Wallace and Gromit film from 1989, Gromit and Wallace build a rocket in their cellar and fly to the Moon, because Wallace has heard it is made of cheese.",
    "K-9 is a robot dog in the British television series Doctor Who, first appearing in 1977. He travels through space and time with the Doctor and has a laser gun in his nose.",
    "In May 1969, the Apollo 10 astronauts nicknamed their lunar module Snoopy and their command module Charlie Brown, after the characters in the Peanuts cartoon strip. NASA still gives a Silver Snoopy award to workers who help keep astronauts safe.",
    "Space Buddies is a 2009 Disney film about five Golden Retriever puppies who stow away on a rocket and end up on a mission to the Moon.",
    "In the Swedish film My Life as a Dog, from 1985, a boy comforts himself when things go wrong by thinking about Laika, the space dog, telling himself that at least he is luckier than her.",
  ],
  "Dog sayings": [
    "Something that looks like a dog's dinner is a mess. The phrase is British, from the 1930s. At first, to be dressed up like a dog's dinner meant to be showily overdressed.",
    "To work like a dog means to work very hard. Working dogs such as sheepdogs and cart dogs were known for labouring all day, so the phrase has been used for hundreds of years.",
    "If you are dog-tired, you are completely worn out, like a dog after a long day of chasing and hunting.",
    "Myth: It's raining cats and dogs because animals slid off thatched roofs in heavy rain. The truth: There is no evidence for this. Nobody knows where the phrase comes from, and it has been used since at least the 1600s.",
    "Let sleeping dogs lie means do not stir up trouble. The idea is very old: the English poet Geoffrey Chaucer wrote, in about 1380, that it is not good to wake a sleeping dog.",
    "You can't teach an old dog new tricks is one of the oldest English sayings. A farming book from 1523 by John Fitzherbert warned that a dog must learn when it is a puppy.",
    "The dog days are the hottest days of summer. The Romans linked them to Sirius, the Dog Star, which rises with the sun in July and August.",
    "Every dog has its day means everyone gets their moment of luck. Shakespeare used it in Hamlet, and the idea goes back even further.",
    "Barking up the wrong tree means looking in the wrong place. It comes from 1800s America, where hunting dogs sometimes barked at the foot of a tree after the animal they were chasing had escaped into another.",
    "If you are in the doghouse, someone is cross with you. One of the most famous early examples is in Peter Pan, where Mr Darling, ashamed of how he treated the family's dog Nana, lives in her kennel.",
    "A dog-eared book has page corners folded down, like the floppy ears of a dog. People have used the phrase since the 1600s.",
    "Feeling as sick as a dog means feeling very ill. Dogs will eat almost anything, and are often sick afterwards, which may be where the saying came from.",
    "When the tail wags the dog, something small is controlling something much bigger. The phrase comes from 1800s America.",
    "The top dog is the winner, and the underdog is the one expected to lose. The words probably come from dog fights in the 1800s, where one dog ended up on top of the other.",
    "Myth: The name hot dog came from a cartoonist who could not spell dachshund. The truth: Historians have never found the cartoon. The name was probably a joke among American students in the 1890s, suggesting the sausages were made of dog meat.",
    "A dogsbody is someone who does all the boring jobs. In the Royal Navy, dogsbody was slang for pease pudding, and then for junior officers who were given the dullest tasks.",
    "A dog in the manger is someone who will not let others have something they cannot use themselves. It comes from Aesop's fable of a dog lying in a manger of hay, which it could not eat, snapping at the ox that wanted it.",
    "His bark is worse than his bite describes someone who sounds fierce but is really quite gentle, like a dog that barks a lot but would never bite.",
    "Let the dog see the rabbit is a British saying meaning give someone space to get on with the job. It comes from hunting, where a dog needs to see its target before it can chase it.",
    "To see a man about a dog is a polite excuse for leaving without saying why. It appears in an 1866 play, Flying Scud, by Dion Boucicault.",
    "A hangdog look is a guilty, ashamed look, like a dog that knows it has done something wrong.",
    "If something has gone to the dogs, it has fallen into a terrible state. The idea may come from food so bad that it was only fit to be thrown to the dogs.",
    "A dog-eat-dog world is one where everyone is out for themselves. It turns round an old Latin saying that dog does not eat dog.",
    "Love me, love my dog means that if you care about someone, you must accept everything about them. It is recorded in English in 1546, and a Latin version is even older.",
    "A shaggy dog story is a long, rambling joke with a silly ending. The name comes from old jokes about a shaggy dog, popular in the 1930s.",
    "A three dog night is an extremely cold night, so cold you would need three dogs in bed to keep warm. Nobody knows for sure where it comes from.",
    "A doggy bag is a bag for taking leftover restaurant food home. The idea was that the scraps were for the dog, though people usually ate them themselves.",
    "In Cockney rhyming slang, the dog and bone means the telephone. So if someone is on the dog, they are on the phone.",
    "A dog is for life, not just for Christmas was written in 1978 by Clarissa Baldwin of the National Canine Defence League, now Dogs Trust, to stop people giving puppies as presents and then abandoning them.",
    "Why keep a dog and bark yourself? means why pay someone to do a job and then do it yourself. The saying has been used in England since the 1500s.",
    "People who fight like cat and dog argue all the time, because cats and dogs were thought to be natural enemies. In fact, many cats and dogs live together happily.",
    "A dog's breakfast, like a dog's dinner, means a real mess, like a bowl of mixed-up scraps.",
    "To be like a dog with two tails is a British way of saying someone is extremely happy, twice as happy as a dog wagging one tail.",
    "A dog-leg is a sharp bend, in a road, a staircase or a golf course, shaped like the bent back leg of a dog.",
    "Doggerel is clumsy, badly written poetry. Nobody is sure why, but it may come from dog, used to mean poor or worthless.",
    "Someone who is dogged keeps going and never gives up, like a dog that will not stop following a trail.",
    "A sop to Cerberus is a small bribe to keep someone quiet. In the Roman poet Virgil's Aeneid, the three-headed dog Cerberus, guard of the underworld, is calmed with a drugged honey cake so a hero can slip past.",
    "Winston Churchill called his times of deep sadness his black dog. Many people today use the phrase to talk about feeling low, and talking to someone you trust can really help.",
    "Dog Latin is bad, made-up Latin, like a dog trying to talk. Schoolchildren and writers have joked with it for centuries.",
    "On ships, the dog watches are two short shifts in the early evening, each lasting two hours rather than the usual four. They let sailors take turns at different times each day.",
    "A dogfight is a close air battle between fighter planes, twisting and turning like fighting dogs. The word became common in the First World War.",
    "If you have not got a dog's chance, you have no chance at all.",
    "Puppy love is the strong, sweet crush a young person feels, named after the eager affection of puppies.",
    "To be like a dog with a bone means to refuse to let something go, just as a dog will not give up a bone once it has one.",
    "Going off with your tail between your legs means leaving ashamed or defeated, just as a frightened or told-off dog tucks its tail down.",
    "Fit as a butcher's dog is a British saying for someone very fit and healthy, like a butcher's dog, well fed on meat scraps.",
    "Let slip the dogs of war comes from Shakespeare's play Julius Caesar, where Mark Antony cries havoc and imagines war let loose like a pack of fierce dogs.",
    "Some wild plants have dog in their name, such as the dog rose and the dog violet. Here dog seems to mean wild or ordinary, to set them apart from garden flowers.",
    "To dog someone means to follow them everywhere, as a dog follows its owner, or to keep causing them trouble.",
    "Man's best friend became a famous phrase after an American lawyer, George Graham Vest, made a speech in 1870 praising a dog, in a court case about a dog called Old Drum who had been shot.",
  ],
  "Clever dogs and dog intelligence": [
    "In his 1994 book The Intelligence of Dogs, the psychologist Stanley Coren ranked breeds by how fast they learn and obey commands, using reports from obedience judges. The Border Collie came first.",
    "In Stanley Coren's 1994 ranking of how quickly dog breeds learn commands, the top ten were the Border Collie, Poodle, German Shepherd, Golden Retriever, Dobermann, Shetland Sheepdog, Labrador, Papillon, Rottweiler and Australian Cattle Dog.",
    "Stanley Coren found that the quickest-learning dog breeds could understand a new command after fewer than five repetitions, and obey it the first time at least 95 times out of 100.",
    "Stanley Coren said there are three kinds of dog intelligence: instinctive, what a dog was bred to do; adaptive, how well it solves problems on its own; and working and obedience, how well it learns from people. His famous ranking only measured the third.",
    "A Border Collie called Chaser, from South Carolina in the USA, learned the names of 1,022 different toys. Her owner, the psychologist John Pilley, trained her for years and published the results in 2011.",
    "In 2004 scientists reported that a Border Collie in Germany called Rico knew the names of about 200 toys. Given a new name, he could work out which unfamiliar toy it belonged to, a skill children use when learning words.",
    "Only a few dogs are gifted word learners. In a 2021 study, scientists in Hungary found that most dogs struggle to learn the names of toys, but a small number, mostly Border Collies, pick up names after hearing them just a few times.",
    "Dogs are brilliant at following a human pointing finger, even as young puppies. Wolves raised by people find it much harder, which suggests dogs' understanding of us grew during thousands of years living with humans.",
    "Stanley Coren estimated that an average dog can understand around 165 words and signals, and the brightest dogs over 250, similar to a two-year-old child.",
    "In 2016, scientists in Hungary scanned the brains of dogs trained to lie still in an MRI machine. They found dogs process the words we say and the tone we use separately, then combine them, much as people do.",
    "In the USA, the scientist Gregory Berns trained dogs to lie completely still in an MRI scanner while awake, so he could see which parts of their brains lit up when they smelled their owner or were offered a treat.",
    "Dogs seem to understand fairness. In a 2008 study, dogs that saw another dog get a treat for the same trick, while they got nothing, soon stopped doing the trick.",
    "A 2013 study at the University of Portsmouth found that dogs told not to take food were more likely to sneak it when the room was dark, as if they understood that the person could not see them.",
    "When a dog looks guilty after doing something wrong, it may not be feeling guilt at all. A 2009 study found that dogs look guilty when their owner scolds them, whether or not they actually misbehaved.",
    "Dogs can learn by watching. Puppies that watch their mother or another trained dog doing a task often pick it up faster than puppies that learn alone.",
    "Dogs often catch yawns from people, especially from their own owners. Scientists think this may be a sign of how closely dogs pay attention to us.",
    "Dogs can remember where food has been hidden, even after a delay, and some can remember which of several containers it was put in.",
    "Dogs learn their own names, and many also learn the names of other people and pets in the family, and will look at the right one when they hear it.",
    "Some dogs have been taught to press buttons that play recorded words, such as outside or play. Scientists are studying whether the dogs are really using them to communicate or just learning which buttons bring rewards.",
    "A dog's brain is roughly the size of a lemon, but the part that deals with smell is, for its size, often said to be around 40 times bigger than a human's.",
    "Clever breeds can be harder to live with, not easier. A bright working dog like a Border Collie that is bored may invent its own jobs, such as herding children or chewing furniture.",
    "Many dogs can work out simple puzzles, like sliding a lid or lifting a flap to find a treat. Puzzle toys are a popular way to keep clever dogs busy.",
    "Dogs read human body language very well. Many can tell where you are about to go just from the direction you turn your head or body.",
    "Dogs can tell the difference between a happy and an angry human face, even in photographs, according to studies that trained dogs to choose between pictures.",
    "Some scientists think dogs can tell roughly how long their owner has been gone. In a 2011 study, dogs greeted owners more excitedly after two hours apart than after 30 minutes.",
    "Poodles, second in Stanley Coren's ranking, were once trained as circus performers because they learned tricks so quickly.",
    "Stanley Coren's ranking placed the Afghan Hound last. That does not mean it is not clever: sighthounds were bred to hunt on their own, far from their owners, so taking orders was never their job.",
    "Border Collies are bred to watch a shepherd's every signal from far away, which is one reason they are so good at learning commands.",
    "Dogs can learn hand signals as well as spoken words, which is why deaf dogs can be trained just as well as hearing ones.",
    "Some dogs learn to open doors, fridges and even gates without being taught, simply by watching people do it.",
    "The German Shepherd, third in Stanley Coren's ranking, is one of the most common police dogs in the world, partly because it learns so many different tasks.",
    "Dogs are one of the few animals known to understand that when a person looks at something, there may be something interesting there.",
    "In experiments, dogs often give up on an impossible puzzle and look to their owner for help, while wolves tend to keep trying on their own. Scientists think living with people taught dogs to ask for help.",
    "Dogs can learn to tell toys apart by their shape, and by colour too, as long as the colours are ones dogs can see, such as blue and yellow.",
    "A dog's intelligence is not just about obeying. A sheepdog working a flock on its own is making dozens of decisions a minute.",
    "The Family Dog Project at Eötvös Loránd University in Budapest, started in 1994, is one of the world's leading research groups studying how dogs think.",
    "Dogs remember people and other dogs they have not seen for a long time, often greeting them with obvious excitement.",
    "Scientists have found that dogs can learn a new trick simply by watching a person do it and then being asked to do it, in a method called Do As I Do.",
    "Dogs appear to understand some simple number differences. In a 2002 study, dogs looked longer at a screen when the number of treats shown did not add up.",
    "Training a dog with rewards, such as treats, toys and praise, is known to work better than punishment, and makes dogs more willing to try new things.",
    "The Labrador is seventh in Stanley Coren's ranking, and its mix of brains and gentle nature is why it is so often chosen as a guide and assistance dog.",
    "Dogs can learn routines so well that they seem to know when it is time for a walk or dinner, even without a clock.",
    "Some dogs can tell when their owner is about to leave the house just from small clues, like picking up keys or putting on shoes.",
    "Dogs can be taught to understand dozens of commands, but they learn best with short, fun training sessions, usually only a few minutes at a time.",
    "In one study, dogs chose to follow the advice of a person who had helped them find food before, rather than a person who had tricked them.",
    "Clever dogs are not only big working breeds. The Papillon, a tiny toy spaniel, came eighth in Stanley Coren's ranking of quick learners.",
    "Some dogs learn to use the toilet on command, to push lift buttons, and even to turn lights on and off, as part of their training as assistance dogs.",
    "Many sheepdog handlers use whistles, and a well-trained sheepdog can learn a different whistle for each of many commands, such as go left, go right, stop and walk on.",
    "Dogs recognise their owners by smell, sight and sound, and can pick out their owner's voice from among other voices.",
    "A dog's sense of smell helps it think. Dogs often solve problems by sniffing first, working out where something is from the smell before they look.",
  ],
  "Dog science": [
    "A dog's nose print is unique, just like a human fingerprint. The pattern of ridges and bumps on its nose is different on every dog.",
    "Dogs can smell with each nostril separately, which helps them work out which direction a smell is coming from.",
    "When a dog sniffs, air goes two ways. Some goes to the lungs, and some goes to a special area at the back of the nose used only for smelling.",
    "When a dog breathes out, the air leaves through slits at the side of its nose, so it does not blow away the smells it is sniffing in front of it.",
    "Dogs have a special smell organ in the roof of the mouth, called the vomeronasal organ or Jacobson's organ, which helps them detect chemical signals from other animals.",
    "Dogs are not colour-blind, but they see fewer colours than we do. They mainly see blues and yellows, and red looks brownish or grey to them.",
    "A red ball on green grass can be hard for a dog to see, because to a dog both colours look similar. A blue or yellow toy is much easier for them to spot.",
    "Dogs see better than us in dim light. A layer at the back of their eyes, called the tapetum lucidum, reflects light, which is why their eyes glow in the dark in photos.",
    "Dogs have a third eyelid, a thin extra layer in the corner of each eye that sweeps across to keep the eye clean and moist.",
    "Dogs can hear much higher sounds than people, up to about 45,000 vibrations a second, which is why they can hear dog whistles that we cannot.",
    "A dog has around 18 muscles in each ear, which lets it swivel and tilt its ears to catch sounds.",
    "Puppies are born with their eyes and ears closed. Their eyes usually open at around two weeks old, and their ears soon after.",
    "A dog is pregnant for about 63 days, around nine weeks, before its puppies are born.",
    "Puppies have 28 baby teeth, which fall out and are replaced by 42 adult teeth, usually by the time they are about six or seven months old.",
    "Dogs mostly cool down by panting, not sweating. They do have some sweat glands, mainly in the pads of their paws.",
    "A dog's normal body temperature is about 38 to 39°C, a little warmer than a human's.",
    "Dogs dream. Like people, they have a stage of sleep called REM sleep, when you may see their paws twitch, their eyes flicker or hear little yips.",
    "Dogs have far fewer taste buds than people: about 1,700, compared with about 9,000 in a human.",
    "Dogs have taste buds for water at the tip of the tongue, which become more sensitive after they eat salty or sugary food, perhaps to make them want to drink.",
    "When a dog laps water, it curls the tip of its tongue backwards to make a scoop, and flicks water up into its mouth so fast the eye can barely see it.",
    "A wet dog can shake off about 70 per cent of the water in its fur in around four seconds, according to scientists at Georgia Tech in the USA.",
    "Whiskers are not just for looks. A dog's whiskers are very sensitive and help it feel things close to its face, especially in the dark.",
    "A study in 2007 found that dogs wag their tails more to the right when they see something they like, such as their owner, and more to the left when they see something that worries them.",
    "Chocolate is poisonous to dogs. It contains a chemical called theobromine that dogs cannot break down quickly, and dark chocolate is the most dangerous.",
    "Grapes, raisins, onions and garlic can all make dogs very ill, so they should never be given to dogs as treats.",
    "The old rule that one dog year equals seven human years is not accurate. Dogs grow up very fast in their first year or two, then age more slowly.",
    "Small dogs usually live longer than big dogs. A tiny breed may live to 15 or more, while some giant breeds live only 8 to 10 years.",
    "Dogs can see movement very well, even far away, which helped their ancestors spot prey. They are less good at seeing still, detailed objects.",
    "Most dogs have a wider field of view than people, about 240 degrees compared with our 180, because their eyes sit more to the sides of their head.",
    "Dogs walk on their toes. The part of a dog's back leg that looks like a backwards knee is really its ankle.",
    "The dewclaw is a small extra toe high on the inside of a dog's leg, a bit like a thumb, that does not touch the ground.",
    "The tough pads on a dog's paws have a layer of fat inside, which helps protect them from cold and rough ground.",
    "Dogs circle round before lying down. Scientists think this may be an instinct from wild ancestors, who flattened grass to make a comfortable bed and checked for danger.",
    "A dog's heart beats faster than ours when it is small. A small dog's heart may beat around 100 to 140 times a minute, while a big dog's is slower.",
    "All dogs belong to the same species, from a tiny Chihuahua to a giant Great Dane, and they can have puppies together.",
    "There are more than 340 dog breeds recognised around the world, making the dog one of the most varied animals on Earth.",
    "Scientists have found that a single gene, IGF1, plays a big part in whether a dog is small or large.",
    "Dogs have a much better sense of smell than sight. Scientists estimate a dog's sense of smell is between 10,000 and 100,000 times better than ours.",
    "Some dogs are born without tails, or with very short ones, such as some Pembroke Welsh Corgis and Old English Sheepdogs, because of a natural gene.",
    "Dogs' wet noses help them smell. A thin layer of mucus catches tiny scent particles from the air.",
    "Dogs cannot see the colour red well, so the orange of a traffic cone or a red toy looks dull and brownish to them.",
    "A puppy's paws can give a hint of how big it will grow. Puppies with very large paws for their size often grow into big dogs, though it is not a perfect rule.",
    "Dogs sleep a lot, often 12 to 14 hours a day, and puppies and older dogs sleep even more.",
    "Dogs have scent glands between their toes. When they scratch the ground after going to the toilet, they may be leaving extra scent messages for other dogs.",
    "A dog's sense of hearing can pick up sounds about four times farther away than a person can.",
    "Trained dogs can often tell identical twins apart by smell, something people cannot do.",
    "Scientists in Sweden found that dogs, unlike wolves, have extra copies of a gene that helps them digest starch, a sign that early dogs lived alongside farmers and ate their scraps.",
    "Research at the University of Portsmouth found that dogs have a special muscle above the eyes that lets them raise their inner eyebrows, making puppy-dog eyes. Wolves do not have it.",
    "Dogs can sense changes in the air before a storm, such as falling air pressure, and some become restless or hide before thunder starts.",
    "The Basenji, an African breed, does not bark in the usual way. Because of the shape of its throat, it makes an unusual yodelling sound instead.",
  ],
  "UK dog charities": [
    "The RSPCA is the oldest animal welfare charity in the world. It was founded on 16 June 1824 at a meeting in a London coffee house called Old Slaughter's.",
    "Among the founders of the RSPCA in 1824 were Richard Martin, the MP behind Britain's first law against cruelty to cattle, and William Wilberforce, famous for fighting slavery.",
    "The RSPCA began as the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals. In 1840 Queen Victoria gave it permission to add Royal to its name.",
    "Richard Martin, one of the founders of the RSPCA, was nicknamed Humanity Dick for his kindness to animals. His 1822 law, known as Martin's Act, protected cattle, horses and sheep from cruelty.",
    "RSPCA inspectors investigate cruelty to animals across England and Wales, and the charity also rescues animals and finds them new homes.",
    "Battersea Dogs and Cats Home began in 1860 as the Temporary Home for Lost and Starving Dogs, started by Mary Tealby in a stable yard in Holloway, north London.",
    "Battersea Dogs and Cats Home moved to Battersea, in south London, in 1871, which is how it got its famous name.",
    "Charles Dickens visited the home that became Battersea Dogs and Cats Home and wrote about it in 1862, praising the work of caring for London's lost dogs.",
    "Battersea Dogs and Cats Home started taking in cats in 1883, and Queen Victoria became its patron in 1885.",
    "Battersea Dogs and Cats Home has three centres: its original home in London, and centres at Old Windsor in Berkshire and Brands Hatch in Kent.",
    "Larry, the Downing Street cat, came from Battersea Dogs and Cats Home in 2011. The home said his fame led to a rise in people adopting cats.",
    "Dogs Trust began in 1891 as the National Canine Defence League, founded at the first Crufts dog show by dog lovers who wanted to protect dogs from cruelty.",
    "The National Canine Defence League changed its name to Dogs Trust in 2003. It is now the largest dog welfare charity in the UK.",
    "Dogs Trust is famous for its promise never to put down a healthy dog. Dogs that are hard to rehome can stay in its care for as long as they need.",
    "The famous slogan A dog is for life, not just for Christmas was written in 1978 by Clarissa Baldwin for the National Canine Defence League, now Dogs Trust.",
    "Dogs Trust has around 20 rehoming centres across the UK and Ireland, and also runs dog training classes called Dog School.",
    "Dogs Trust runs the Hope Project, which helps people who are homeless look after their dogs, with free vet care, so they do not have to give them up.",
    "Blue Cross began in 1897 as Our Dumb Friends' League, a charity that first cared for the working horses of London.",
    "Blue Cross got its name from a fund set up in 1912 to help horses caught up in war in the Balkans. The fund's blue cross symbol later became the name of the whole charity.",
    "During the First World War, Blue Cross raised money to care for horses wounded at the front.",
    "Blue Cross opened one of the first animal hospitals in Britain, in Victoria, London, in 1906, to treat the pets of people who could not afford a vet.",
    "Blue Cross runs a pet bereavement support service, a phone and email line for people grieving the loss of a pet, which it started in 1994.",
    "Woodgreen Pets Charity was founded in 1924 by Louisa Snow, who was worried about the many abandoned and injured animals on London's streets after the First World War.",
    "Woodgreen began in a house on Lordship Lane in Wood Green, north London, which gave the charity its name.",
    "In 1987 Woodgreen opened its main centre at Godmanchester in Cambridgeshire, which has since become one of the UK's largest animal rehoming centres.",
    "Woodgreen became famous on television as the rehoming centre in the Channel 4 series The Dog House, which follows dogs being matched with new families.",
    "Woodgreen was once called Wood Green Animal Shelters, then Wood Green, The Animals Charity, before becoming Woodgreen Pets Charity.",
    "The National Animal Welfare Trust, known as NAWT, became an independent charity in 1971. Its roots go back to 1958, when a dog rescue was set up to stop dogs bought at markets being sent to laboratories.",
    "The National Animal Welfare Trust was first called the Animal Welfare Trust. The word National was added in 1996, for its 25th birthday.",
    "The National Animal Welfare Trust's first rescue centre was a boarding kennels in Watford, Hertfordshire, bought in 1981. It is still the charity's headquarters.",
    "The National Animal Welfare Trust runs Trindledown Farm, described as the UK's only retirement home for elderly pets.",
    "The National Animal Welfare Trust has rehoming centres in Hertfordshire, Essex, Berkshire, Cornwall and Bedfordshire, and cares for about 1,500 animals a year.",
    "The National Animal Welfare Trust was the first UK rescue to use the Open Paw programme, which gives rescue animals training and things to do so they settle more easily into new homes.",
    "Guide Dogs, the British charity, breeds most of its own puppies at its National Centre near Leamington Spa in Warwickshire.",
    "Guide Dogs relies on volunteers called puppy raisers, who look after a guide dog puppy in their own home for about its first year.",
    "Guide Dogs is paid for almost entirely by donations. It says a guide dog partnership costs tens of thousands of pounds over the dog's life, but is free to the person who needs it.",
    "Guide Dogs does not only train guide dogs. It also offers help such as sighted guiding, where trained volunteers walk with a person with sight loss.",
    "The PDSA, the People's Dispensary for Sick Animals, was founded in 1917 by Maria Dickin to give free vet care to the pets of poor families. It later created the Dickin Medal for brave animals.",
    "The PDSA's first free animal clinic opened in a basement in Whitechapel, east London, in 1917, with a sign inviting people to bring their sick animals.",
    "Hearing Dogs for Deaf People, founded in 1982, trains dogs to alert deaf people to important sounds. Its dogs wear burgundy coats.",
    "The Kennel Club, founded in 1873, is not a rescue charity, but it keeps Britain's register of pedigree dogs and runs Crufts, the world's largest dog show.",
    "Many UK dog charities encourage new owners to visit and meet a dog more than once before taking it home.",
    "Most UK rescue charities microchip and vaccinate their dogs before rehoming them. By 2016 it had become the law in England, Scotland and Wales that dogs must be microchipped.",
    "Charities that rehome dogs often check a family's home and garden first, to make sure it is safe and suits the dog.",
    "The RSPCA takes in tens of thousands of animals every year, including dogs, cats, rabbits, horses and wildlife.",
    "Battersea Dogs and Cats Home has a long-standing rule to take in any dog or cat in need, whatever its age, breed or health.",
    "The RSPCA has been protecting animals for more than 200 years, since it was founded in 1824.",
    "Blue Cross runs animal hospitals in London and elsewhere, which treat the pets of people who cannot afford private vet bills.",
    "Dogs Trust runs free and low-cost neutering and microchipping schemes to help reduce the number of unwanted puppies.",
    "Many UK dog charities rely on volunteers to walk dogs, clean kennels, foster animals at home and raise money in charity shops.",
  ],
  "Wolves and dogs": [
    "Every dog alive today descends from wolves. But dogs did not come from today's grey wolves: both come from an older kind of wolf that has since died out.",
    "Scientists think dogs split from wolves somewhere between about 15,000 and 40,000 years ago. Dogs were the first animal ever to be tamed by people, long before cows, sheep or horses.",
    "One of the oldest known dogs was found buried with two people at Bonn-Oberkassel in Germany. It lived about 14,000 years ago, and seems to have been cared for when it was ill.",
    "Nobody knows exactly how wolves became dogs. One idea is that friendlier wolves hung around human camps for scraps, and over time became tamer and tamer.",
    "Dogs and wolves are so closely related that they can still have puppies together. Their DNA is almost identical.",
    "Wolves once lived all over Britain. They were hunted until they disappeared, probably from England by around 1500 and from Scotland by the 1700s.",
    "According to an old story, the English King Edgar demanded 300 wolf skins every year from a Welsh king in the 900s as tribute, to help rid the land of wolves.",
    "Legend says the last wolf in Scotland was killed in 1743 by a hunter called MacQueen. Historians are not sure the story is true.",
    "Wolves live in family groups called packs, usually a mother and father wolf and their young from the past few years.",
    "Myth: Wolf packs are ruled by a fierce alpha wolf who fights its way to the top. The truth: In the wild, a pack is usually just a family, led by the parents. The alpha idea came from studies of unrelated wolves kept together in captivity.",
    "Wolves howl to keep in touch with their pack and warn other wolves away from their territory. A howl can be heard many kilometres away.",
    "Adult wolves rarely bark, but dogs bark a lot. Scientists think barking became more common in dogs because it was useful to people as a warning.",
    "Dogs can read human faces and follow our pointing fingers much more easily than wolves can, even wolves raised by people from puppies.",
    "Wolves and dogs have the same number of adult teeth, 42, but wolves' teeth are bigger and their jaws are stronger.",
    "Many dogs have floppy ears, curly tails and patches of white, but wild wolves almost never do. Scientists call these changes, which appear in many tamed animals, the domestication syndrome.",
    "In a famous experiment in Russia starting in 1959, scientists bred foxes for tameness. After many generations, some tame foxes developed floppy ears, curly tails and patchy coats, just like dogs.",
    "Wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in the USA in 1995, after being wiped out there. Their return changed how deer and elk behaved, and scientists are still studying how far the effects spread.",
    "Some dog breeds were created by crossing dogs with wolves, such as the Czechoslovakian Wolfdog and the Saarloos Wolfdog.",
    "Huskies and Malamutes look a lot like wolves, and some Arctic sled dogs carry a little extra DNA from an ancient Siberian wolf. But they are still dogs, not part-wolves.",
    "Wolves hunt as a team, chasing large prey such as deer, and can run at up to about 60km/h in short bursts.",
    "Wolf pups are born blind and deaf in a den, and stay there for several weeks while the adults bring them food.",
    "The grey wolf is the largest member of the dog family in the wild. Big males can weigh over 50kg.",
    "Wolves appear in many famous stories, from Little Red Riding Hood to the Three Little Pigs, usually as villains. Real wild wolves are shy and usually avoid people.",
    "In the legend of how Rome began, the twin brothers Romulus and Remus were saved as babies by a she-wolf, who fed them with her milk.",
    "Wild wolves usually live only about 6 to 8 years, while pet dogs often live much longer, thanks to regular food, shelter and vets.",
  ],
  "Dogs in archaeology": [
    "The oldest known dog in Britain lived in Gough's Cave in Cheddar Gorge, Somerset, about 14,300 years ago. DNA tests on a small jawbone, published in 2026, proved it was a dog and not a wolf.",
    "Gough's Cave in Cheddar Gorge is famous for two ancient finds: Cheddar Man, the oldest near-complete human skeleton found in Britain, and now Britain's oldest known dog.",
    "The Gough's Cave dog jawbone was found in the collections of the Natural History Museum, where it had sat for years. A researcher, Dr William Marsh, tested it by chance during his studies and found it was a dog.",
    "In 2026 scientists announced the oldest genetic evidence of dogs yet found: puppies from Pınarbaşı in Turkey, about 15,800 years old. It pushed back the earliest DNA proof of dogs by more than 5,000 years.",
    "The ancient dogs from Gough's Cave in England, Kesslerloch in Switzerland and Pınarbaşı in Turkey had very similar DNA, even though they lived around 2,500 miles apart, among completely different peoples.",
    "Dogs were living with people across Europe by at least 14,000 years ago, long before anyone farmed, kept sheep or tamed cats. Dogs came first.",
    "At ʿAin Mallaha in Israel, archaeologists found the 12,000-year-old grave of a woman buried with her hand resting on a puppy, one of the most famous finds showing how close people and dogs had become.",
    "At Hayonim Terrace in Israel, a person was buried with two dogs about 12,000 to 13,000 years ago, showing that dogs were already part of family life there.",
    "At Bonn-Oberkassel in Germany, a dog was buried with two people about 14,000 years ago. Its bones show it had been very ill as a puppy, and it could only have survived if people nursed it.",
    "Archaeologists can tell an ancient dog from a wolf by its bones. Early dogs usually have shorter snouts, wider skulls and teeth that are more crowded together.",
    "Today scientists can also tell dogs from wolves using ancient DNA, taken from bones or teeth, which is how many old puzzles about early dogs have finally been solved.",
    "Some skulls from caves in Belgium and Siberia, around 33,000 to 36,000 years old, look partly like dogs. Scientists still argue about whether they were early dogs or just unusual wolves.",
    "At Star Carr, a famous Stone Age site near Scarborough in North Yorkshire, about 11,000 years old, archaeologists found the bones of dogs that lived with the hunters there.",
    "Studies of chemicals in the bones of ancient dogs show they often ate the same kinds of food as the people they lived with, such as fish, which suggests they were fed scraps or shared meals.",
    "Ancient Egyptians buried millions of dogs. The Dog Catacombs at Saqqara, near a temple of the dog-headed god Anubis, are thought to hold around 8 million animal mummies, most of them dogs.",
    "Many of the mummified dogs at Saqqara in Egypt were only hours or days old, which suggests puppies were bred in large numbers to be offered to the god Anubis.",
    "The catacombs of dog mummies at Saqqara in Egypt were first recorded in the 1800s, but were only studied in detail much more recently, with the findings published in 2015.",
    "Ancient Egyptians sometimes gave their dogs names and fine collars. One decorated leather dog collar was found in the tomb of Maiherpri, a royal servant, in the Valley of the Kings.",
    "At Ashkelon, an ancient city on the coast of Israel, archaeologists found a cemetery of more than 1,000 dogs, buried carefully one by one about 2,500 years ago. Nobody is sure why.",
    "When Mount Vesuvius erupted in AD 79, a guard dog in Pompeii was still chained up. Archaeologists poured plaster into the space its body left in the ash, making a cast that shows its final moments.",
    "At Pompeii, some houses had floor mosaics of dogs with the words Cave canem, meaning beware of the dog, near the front door, a warning to visitors.",
    "Roman roof tiles found at sites in Britain, such as Vindolanda near Hadrian's Wall, sometimes carry dog paw prints, left when a dog walked across the tiles while they were still soft and drying.",
    "The Romans in Britain kept many kinds of dog, from big hunting and guard dogs to tiny lapdogs. Some small dogs were buried with care, suggesting they were much-loved pets.",
    "In Iron Age Britain, over 2,000 years ago, dogs were sometimes buried in pits at hillforts and farms. Archaeologists think some were placed there as offerings.",
    "British hunting dogs were famous in the ancient world. Roman writers said dogs from Britain were exported to Rome to be used for hunting.",
    "Viking chiefs were sometimes buried with their dogs. The great ship burials at Gokstad and Oseberg in Norway, from the 800s, both included the remains of dogs.",
    "In ancient China, dogs were sometimes buried in small pits under a person's coffin, perhaps to guard them in the afterlife. This was common in the Shang dynasty, over 3,000 years ago.",
    "On Zhokhov Island in the Siberian Arctic, archaeologists found the remains of dogs about 9,500 years old. Studies suggest people there were already breeding dogs to pull sledges.",
    "Dogs travelled to the Americas with the first people who crossed from Siberia. Some of the earliest dog burials in North America, at the Koster site in Illinois, are about 10,000 years old.",
    "In Peru, the Chiribaya people buried some of their dogs about 1,000 years ago in their own graves, wrapped in blankets and with food, as if they were family members.",
    "In ancient Japan, during the Jōmon period, dogs were carefully buried near people, and some show healed injuries, suggesting they were looked after when hurt.",
    "The Aztecs of Mexico kept a hairless dog called the Xoloitzcuintli, and its remains have been found in ancient burials. It was linked to Xolotl, a god who guided the dead.",
    "Dog bones found in ancient sites often show wear and damage from hard work, such as pulling loads, giving archaeologists clues about the jobs dogs did.",
    "Archaeologists use radiocarbon dating to work out how old dog bones are. It measures a type of carbon that slowly disappears after an animal dies, like a very slow clock.",
    "Ancient DNA shows that the dogs of Britain have changed many times over thousands of years, as new peoples arrived and brought their own dogs with them.",
    "Some ancient dog burials in Europe include grave goods, such as beads or tools, the same kinds of things placed in human graves.",
    "In Sweden, at a Stone Age cemetery called Skateholm, about 7,000 years old, some dogs were buried in their own graves, one even with grave goods like a person.",
    "The first farmers who came to Britain about 6,000 years ago brought dogs with them, and dog bones are found at many early farming sites.",
    "In ancient Greece and Rome, dogs sometimes had their own gravestones with carved messages from their owners, praising them as loyal and brave.",
    "Dogs appear in some of the oldest rock art in the world. Carvings in Saudi Arabia, thought to be at least 8,000 years old, show hunters with dogs, some apparently on leads.",
    "In medieval Britain, dog bones found in towns show a huge range of sizes, from small house dogs to big hounds, much like the variety of dogs we see today.",
    "Archaeologists study dog teeth to learn about their health. Tooth wear, disease and missing teeth can show what a dog ate and how old it was.",
    "Some ancient dogs were buried curled up as if asleep, placed gently in their graves by the people who loved them.",
    "Ancient dog footprints have been found alongside human footprints in several places, preserved in mud that later hardened, showing people and dogs walking side by side.",
    "Ancient paintings in Egyptian tombs often show dogs on leads walking with their owners or joining hunts, showing how much the Egyptians valued them.",
    "When a dog is found in a human grave, archaeologists look carefully at how it was placed. A careful burial suggests the dog was loved and valued, not just thrown away.",
    "Archaeologists sometimes find evidence that ancient people cared for old or disabled dogs, such as healed broken bones or dogs that lived long after losing teeth.",
    "Dog bones are some of the most common animal remains found at ancient human settlements, because wherever people lived, dogs lived too.",
    "Scientists can study ancient dog DNA to find out what colour the dogs were. Some very early dogs seem to have had coats similar to wolves.",
    "The discovery that dogs lived with people for thousands of years before farming shows that the friendship between humans and dogs is the oldest partnership we have with any animal.",
    "At Cuween Hill, a Stone Age tomb on Orkney about 4,500 years old, archaeologists found the skulls of 24 dogs. Some think the people who built it saw the dog as a symbol of their community.",
    "In 2019 experts in Scotland made a lifelike model of the head of one of the Cuween Hill dogs from Orkney, using its 4,500-year-old skull. It looked a lot like a European grey wolf, about the size of a large Collie.",
    "When Henry VIII's warship the Mary Rose sank in 1545, a dog was aboard. Its skeleton was found in the carpenter's cabin, and the dog has been nicknamed Hatch.",
    "DNA tests on Hatch, the Mary Rose ship's dog, showed he was a young male, probably with a brown coat, and scientists think he was a mongrel of the sort kept on ships to catch rats.",
    "At Lydney Park in Gloucestershire, a Roman temple to a healing god called Nodens was full of dog figures. The most famous is a small bronze dog, now known as the Lydney Dog, which looks like a wolfhound.",
    "Archaeologists think dogs were linked with healing at the Roman temple at Lydney Park, perhaps because people believed a dog licking a wound could help it heal.",
    "The Jennings Dog, a large Roman marble statue of a Molossian hound, is in the British Museum in London. It was copied from a Greek bronze statue made more than 2,000 years ago.",
    "Two Roman marble statues of greyhounds playing together, known as the Townley Greyhounds, are in the British Museum. They show how the Romans admired fast, slim hunting dogs.",
    "In ancient Egypt, a royal guard dog called Abuwtiyuw was buried with honours near the Great Pyramid at Giza, on the orders of a king. An inscription about him is one of the earliest records of a named dog.",
    "In the tomb of the young Egyptian king Tutankhamun, discovered in 1922, a large carved black figure of the dog-headed god Anubis lay on top of a golden chest, guarding the treasure.",
    "Ancient Egyptian tomb paintings sometimes show dogs sitting under their owners' chairs, just as pet dogs do today.",
    "In ancient China, people made clay model dogs to be buried with the dead, especially during the Han dynasty, about 2,000 years ago. Many are kept in museums today.",
    "Beside Lake Baikal in Siberia, archaeologists found a dog buried about 7,000 years ago in a grave just like a person's, with objects placed beside it.",
    "In a 2020 study of ancient dog DNA, scientists found that by about 11,000 years ago, there were already at least five different families of dogs around the world.",
    "The same 2020 study found that when farmers spread into Europe thousands of years ago, their dogs mixed with the dogs already living there, so modern European dogs have more than one ancient ancestry.",
    "Dingoes reached Australia about 3,500 years ago, carried by people from Asia. The oldest dingo bones found so far, from Madura Cave in Western Australia, are about 3,300 years old.",
    "Some Native American peoples used dogs to pull a travois, a frame of two poles dragged along the ground, carrying tents and belongings when they moved camp.",
    "The Coast Salish people of north-west America once kept a woolly dog whose fur was sheared like a sheep's to weave blankets. A study in 2023 looked at the fur of one of these dogs, called Mutton, kept since 1859.",
    "Archaeologists can prove that dogs lived at an ancient camp even when no dog bones are left. Other animal bones may carry gnaw marks from dogs' teeth.",
    "Fossilised dog poo, called coprolites, is found at some ancient sites. Scientists study it to learn what dogs ate and what parasites they carried.",
    "Cats were tamed much later than dogs. The oldest known pet cat, found buried with a person on the island of Cyprus, is about 9,500 years old, thousands of years after the first dogs.",
    "Sheep, goats and cows were first tamed about 10,000 to 11,000 years ago, so dogs had already been living with people for thousands of years before any farm animals.",
    "At some Roman sites in Britain, dog skulls and skeletons have been found at the bottom of old wells. Archaeologists think they may have been placed there on purpose, perhaps as offerings.",
    "In 1881 a pet cemetery began in the garden of a lodge at Hyde Park in London. Over the next years, around 300 pets, mostly dogs, were buried there with small gravestones.",
    "When archaeologists dig, they often sieve the soil through fine mesh. That is how tiny bones, such as those of newborn puppies, are found.",
    "Archaeologists can estimate a dog's age from its bones and teeth. In young dogs, the ends of the leg bones have not yet fused to the rest of the bone.",
    "The scientific study of animal bones from ancient sites is called zooarchaeology. Dogs are among the most important animals zooarchaeologists study.",
    "Early dogs were probably about the size of a modern Collie or large Labrador. Very small and very large dogs only appeared much later.",
    "Some scientists think wolves became dogs by scavenging around human camps, a theory known as the commensal route. Others think people took in wolf cubs on purpose. The truth may be a mixture of both.",
    "Dog skeletons from Stone Age sites sometimes show broken bones that healed, suggesting injured dogs were looked after until they recovered.",
    "In ancient Greece, vase paintings often show dogs, from small lapdogs sitting with children to hunting hounds chasing hares.",
    "Archaeologists found that some ancient people in the Arctic fed their sledge dogs on seal and fish, from chemical signs left in the dogs' bones.",
    "Ancient dog bones from Britain show that big, strong hunting dogs were kept here long before the Romans came, which may be why the Romans prized British dogs.",
    "Anglo-Saxon laws in England, written over 1,000 years ago, set out fines for people whose dogs bit or killed someone, showing dogs were an everyday part of life.",
    "Medieval manuscripts, hand-written and painted books, are full of pictures of dogs: hunting hounds, lapdogs on laps, and dogs curled up at people's feet.",
    "On many medieval tombs in British churches, a carved dog lies at the feet of a stone knight or lady, often thought to stand for loyalty.",
    "Dog bones from medieval castles in Britain include both big hunting hounds and small lapdogs, showing that noble families kept dogs for both sport and company.",
    "Ancient dog collars are rare finds, because leather rots. Metal parts sometimes survive, such as studs, rings and name tags.",
    "Some Roman writers gave advice on naming dogs, suggesting short names that were easy to call, such as Ferox, meaning fierce, or Lupa, meaning she-wolf.",
    "Ancient footprints of a child and a dog-like animal, found in the Chauvet Cave in France, may be about 26,000 years old. Experts still debate whether the animal was a dog or a wolf.",
    "Many archaeologists say that, around the world, more ancient dogs were carefully buried than any other animal, a sign of how special dogs were to people.",
    "In ancient Mesopotamia, in what is now Iraq, dogs were linked with Gula, a goddess of healing, and many small dog figures were left at her temples.",
    "At the ancient city of Isin in Iraq, archaeologists found more than 30 dog burials near the temple of Gula, the goddess of healing, about 3,000 years old.",
    "Some ancient Egyptian dogs were given their own coffins and mummified carefully, wrapped in linen just like people.",
    "Ancient Egyptians wrote down the names of some of their dogs. Recorded names include ones said to mean Brave One and Good Herdsman.",
    "In Roman times, dogs were often shown on oil lamps, coins and pottery, which archaeologists find in huge numbers at Roman sites, including in Britain.",
    "Archaeologists found that some Bronze Age people in Russia used dogs in special ceremonies. At a site called Krasnosamarskoe, many dog bones were found, from dogs of all ages.",
    "The age of a dog when it died can show how it was treated. Finding many old dogs at a site suggests they were cared for into old age, rather than used and discarded.",
    "Tiny bones of lapdogs found at Roman sites across Europe show that people were already breeding very small dogs as pets around 2,000 years ago.",
    "Every new dig that finds dog bones buried beside people adds to the evidence that the friendship between humans and dogs is at least 14,000 years old, and probably older.",
  ],
  "Dogs in British literature and music": [
    "The only dog to appear on stage in a Shakespeare play is Crab, in The Two Gentlemen of Verona. His owner, the servant Launce, complains that Crab did not shed a single tear when the family said goodbye.",
    "In Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream, the workmen putting on a play give the character Moonshine a lantern, a thorn bush and a dog to carry, to stand for the man in the moon.",
    "In Geoffrey Chaucer's The Canterbury Tales, from the late 1300s, the Prioress spoils her little dogs with roast meat, milk and fine white bread.",
    "Sir Walter Scott, the Scottish author, loved his huge Deerhound, Maida. A statue of Maida lies at Scott's feet in the Scott Monument in Edinburgh.",
    "The Dandie Dinmont Terrier is named after a character in a novel. Dandie Dinmont is a farmer who keeps terriers in Sir Walter Scott's book Guy Mannering, published in 1815.",
    "Lord Byron, the poet, loved his Newfoundland dog, Boatswain. When Boatswain died in 1808, Byron built him a grand monument at Newstead Abbey in Nottinghamshire and wrote a poem, Epitaph to a Dog.",
    "Lord Byron wanted to be buried beside his dog Boatswain, and said so in his will, but he was eventually buried in a church in Hucknall instead.",
    "Elizabeth Barrett Browning's spaniel Flush was stolen by dog thieves in London more than once, and each time she paid a ransom to get him back.",
    "Elizabeth Barrett Browning wrote a poem, To Flush, My Dog, in 1843, praising the Cocker Spaniel who kept her company when she was ill.",
    "Charles Dickens kept many dogs, including a Saint Bernard called Linda, a big dog called Sultan, and a little Pomeranian called Mrs Bouncer.",
    "In Charles Dickens's novel Dombey and Son, the lonely Florence Dombey is given a scruffy, loving dog called Diogenes.",
    "In Charles Dickens's David Copperfield, Dora, whom David marries, has a tiny spoilt dog called Jip.",
    "In Charles Dickens's The Cricket on the Hearth, from 1845, the Peerybingle family's dog is called Boxer.",
    "In Jane Austen's Mansfield Park, from 1814, lazy Lady Bertram spends her days on the sofa with her pug.",
    "Emily Brontë, author of Wuthering Heights, had a big, fierce but loyal dog called Keeper. When she died in 1848, Keeper walked behind her coffin at the funeral.",
    "Anne Brontë, the youngest of the Brontë sisters, had a King Charles Spaniel called Flossy.",
    "In Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre, Mr Rochester's big black and white dog is called Pilot. Jane first mistakes him for a Gytrash, a ghostly dog from northern English folklore.",
    "The author Thomas Hardy had a wire-haired Fox Terrier called Wessex, famous for biting visitors to Hardy's home in Dorset. Hardy wrote a poem about him when he died.",
    "In Arthur Conan Doyle's The Sign of Four, Sherlock Holmes borrows a sniffer dog called Toby, an ugly, long-haired, lop-eared mongrel with a wonderful nose, to follow a trail across London.",
    "In the Sherlock Holmes story Silver Blaze, the clue is the curious incident of the dog in the night-time: the dog did nothing, because it knew the thief.",
    "Mark Haddon's 2003 novel The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-Time, about a boy investigating the death of a neighbour's dog, takes its title from a Sherlock Holmes story.",
    "In Jerome K. Jerome's comic book Three Men in a Boat, from 1889, three friends row up the River Thames with a Fox Terrier called Montmorency, who causes trouble all the way.",
    "J. M. Barrie, who wrote Peter Pan, owned a Saint Bernard called Porthos before his Newfoundland, Luath, who was the model for Nana.",
    "Rudyard Kipling wrote a whole book from a dog's point of view: Thy Servant a Dog, from 1930, told by an Aberdeen Terrier called Boots.",
    "In Rudyard Kipling's Just So story The Cat That Walked by Himself, the Wild Dog is the first wild animal to come to the cave, and becomes First Friend.",
    "Rudyard Kipling wrote a famous poem, The Power of the Dog, warning readers not to give their heart to a dog to tear, because dogs live such short lives.",
    "In Rudyard Kipling's The Jungle Book, the boy Mowgli is raised by a family of wolves, and learns the Law of the Jungle from their wise pack.",
    "In the Jeeves stories by P. G. Wodehouse, Stiffy Byng owns Bartholomew, a fierce Aberdeen Terrier who chases Bertie Wooster on to the top of a wardrobe.",
    "In Enid Blyton's Famous Five books, Timmy the dog belongs to George and appears in all 21 adventures, often saving the children from villains.",
    "In Enid Blyton's Secret Seven books, the children's dog is Scamper, a golden spaniel.",
    "Agatha Christie dedicated her 1937 mystery Dumb Witness to her own wire-haired terrier, Peter. In the story, a terrier called Bob is a key part of the mystery.",
    "In George Orwell's Animal Farm, the pig Napoleon secretly raises nine puppies to become his fierce guard dogs, and uses them to take control of the farm.",
    "Beatrix Potter's The Tale of Jemima Puddle-Duck features Kep, a wise Collie who rescues the silly duck from a sly fox.",
    "In Beatrix Potter's The Tale of the Pie and the Patty-Pan, a little dog called Duchess is invited to tea by a cat called Ribby, and worries about the mouse pie.",
    "Michael Morpurgo, the former Children's Laureate, wrote Born to Run, following a Greyhound through many owners, and Shadow, about a Springer Spaniel sniffer dog in Afghanistan.",
    "In the first Harry Potter book, a huge three-headed dog called Fluffy guards a secret trapdoor. Like Cerberus in Greek myth, he falls asleep when music is played.",
    "In the Harry Potter books, Sirius Black can turn into a big black dog. Some characters mistake him for the Grim, an omen of death like the black dogs of British folklore.",
    "In Terry Pratchett's Discworld books, Gaspode the Wonder Dog is a small, smelly, streetwise dog who can talk, though almost nobody believes it.",
    "Dodie Smith, author of The Hundred and One Dalmatians, owned Dalmatians herself. Her first was called Pongo, and at one time she is said to have had nine.",
    "Dodie Smith wrote a sequel to The Hundred and One Dalmatians, called The Starlight Barking, published in 1967, in which all the dogs wake to find the humans will not wake up.",
    "In Richard Adams's The Plague Dogs, from 1977, two dogs, Rowf and Snitter, escape from a research laboratory and try to survive in the fells of the Lake District.",
    "Philippa Pearce's 1962 children's book A Dog So Small is about a boy who longs for a dog so badly that he imagines one, until he finally gets a real puppy.",
    "In Dick King-Smith's The Sheep-Pig, which became the film Babe, a kind Border Collie called Fly adopts the piglet Babe and helps teach him to herd sheep.",
    "Spot, the little puppy in the lift-the-flap books, was created by the British author Eric Hill. The first book, Where's Spot?, came out in 1980.",
    "Kipper, the friendly dog in the picture books and cartoons, was created by the British author and illustrator Mick Inkpen in 1991.",
    "Shirley Hughes's 1977 picture book Dogger is about a boy who loses his favourite toy dog. It won the Kate Greenaway Medal for illustration.",
    "The Yorkshire vet Alf Wight, writing as James Herriot, told funny stories of Tricki Woo, a pampered Pekingese who became ill from too many treats.",
    "In Gerald Durrell's My Family and Other Animals, about growing up on the Greek island of Corfu, his family's faithful dog is called Roger.",
    "The Incredible Journey, from 1961, by the Scottish-born author Sheila Burnford, follows a Labrador, a Bull Terrier and a Siamese cat walking hundreds of miles home.",
    "In Bram Stoker's Dracula, from 1897, the vampire arrives at Whitby in Yorkshire in the shape of a huge dog, which leaps from a shipwreck and runs up the cliff.",
    "In Lewis Carroll's Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, the shrunken Alice meets an enormous puppy and has to dodge its playful paws.",
    "In 1786 the Scottish poet Robert Burns wrote The Twa Dogs, a poem in which two dogs, Caesar and Luath, chat about the lives of rich and poor people.",
    "In 1805 Charles Gough died on the mountain Helvellyn in the Lake District, and his dog stayed by his body for three months. Both William Wordsworth and Walter Scott wrote poems about her loyalty.",
    "The poet Alexander Pope gave the Prince of Wales a puppy with a collar that read: I am his Highness' dog at Kew; Pray tell me, sir, whose dog are you?",
    "The poet Matthew Arnold wrote Geist's Grave in 1881, a poem mourning his family's Dachshund, Geist.",
    "The poet William Cowper wrote several poems about his spaniel, Beau, in the 1790s, including one scolding Beau for killing a young bird.",
    "Robert Louis Stevenson, author of Treasure Island, wrote an essay called The Character of Dogs in 1884, about how dogs behave and feel.",
    "In 1570 Dr John Caius, a physician at Cambridge, wrote the first book about the different kinds of English dogs. It was translated into English as Of English Dogges in 1576.",
    "The Book of St Albans, printed in 1486, contains one of the earliest English lists of kinds of dogs, including the greyhound, the spaniel and the mastiff.",
    "The Master of Game, written between about 1406 and 1413 by Edward, Duke of York, is one of the oldest books in English about hunting, and describes hounds and how to care for them.",
    "Thomas Bewick's A General History of Quadrupeds, from 1790, included beautiful woodcut pictures of many British dog breeds, and helps historians know what old breeds looked like.",
    "In the old Welsh tale Culhwch and Olwen, part of the Mabinogion, King Arthur's hound is called Cafall.",
    "In the Cornish legend of Tristan and Iseult, Tristan's loyal dog is called Husdent.",
    "Rudyard Kipling's story Garm, a Hostage, from 1899, is about a brave, devoted Bull Terrier and the soldier he loves.",
    "Thomas Hardy's poem Dead Wessex the Dog to the Household imagines his dog speaking to the family after his death.",
    "In The Snowman and the Snowdog, made in 2012 as a sequel to Raymond Briggs's The Snowman, a boy builds a snow dog who comes to life.",
    "The Scottish poet Robert Burns named a dog in his poem The Twa Dogs after his own dog, Luath, and J. M. Barrie later named his Newfoundland Luath too.",
    "In Oliver Goldsmith's comic poem Elegy on the Death of a Mad Dog, from 1766, a dog bites a man, but it is the dog that dies.",
    "In the film and stage musical Oliver!, written by the British composer Lionel Bart in 1960, Bill Sikes's dog Bull's-eye appears alongside the orphan Oliver.",
    "In Enid Blyton's Barney mysteries, the children's dog is Loony, a lively, silly spaniel.",
    "Sir Edward Elgar's Enigma Variations, from 1899, include a piece describing a Bulldog called Dan falling into the River Wye, paddling upstream and barking happily on the bank.",
    "The Beatles' song Martha My Dear, from 1968, is named after Paul McCartney's Old English Sheepdog, Martha.",
    "At the very end of the Beatles' song A Day in the Life, Paul McCartney added a very high-pitched sound, said to be too high for people to hear but perfect for dogs.",
    "The Beatles recorded a song called Hey Bulldog for the 1969 film Yellow Submarine, and Paul McCartney barks in it.",
    "How Much Is That Doggie in the Window? was an American song, but Lita Roza's version reached number one in Britain in 1953, making her the first British woman to top the UK charts.",
    "Led Zeppelin's 1971 song Black Dog was named after a black Labrador who wandered around the old house in Hampshire where the band was recording.",
    "Pink Floyd's song Seamus, from 1971, features a dog called Seamus howling along to the music. He belonged to the musician Steve Marriott.",
    "Pink Floyd's 1977 album Animals includes a long song called Dogs, which compares greedy, ruthless people to dogs.",
    "David Bowie's 1974 album Diamond Dogs imagines a gloomy future city roamed by gangs called the Diamond Dogs.",
    "Florence and the Machine's song Dog Days Are Over, released in 2008, became one of the band's most famous hits.",
    "The Bonzo Dog Doo-Dah Band was a wacky British comedy rock band of the 1960s, who appeared in the Beatles' film Magical Mystery Tour.",
    "Ozzy Osbourne's 1983 album and song Bark at the Moon turned the idea of a howling beast into heavy metal.",
    "Kate Bush's 1985 album Hounds of Love takes its title from a song about being chased by love as if by a pack of hounds.",
    "The nursery rhyme Old Mother Hubbard, about a woman who finds her cupboard bare when she goes to fetch her dog a bone, was published in its famous form by Sarah Catherine Martin in 1805.",
    "The nursery rhyme Hark, Hark, the Dogs Do Bark, the beggars are coming to town, is very old. Some think it dates from Tudor times, when bands of wandering beggars roamed England.",
    "In the nursery rhyme Hey Diddle Diddle, when the cow jumps over the moon, the little dog laughs to see such fun.",
    "The children's song about a farmer's dog called Bingo, spelled out B-I-N-G-O, has been sung in Britain for well over 200 years.",
    "In the counting song This Old Man, every verse ends with knick-knack paddywhack, give the dog a bone.",
    "The Cumbrian hunting song D'ye Ken John Peel, from the 1800s, is about a real huntsman and names his hounds, including Ranter, Ringwood, Bellman and True.",
    "In Walter Scott's novel Woodstock, from 1826, the loyal hound Bevis is said to be based on Scott's own Deerhound, Maida.",
  ],
  "Dogs in religion and folklore": [
    "In the Bible, dogs are mentioned around 40 times, but mostly in an unfriendly way, as wild scavengers rather than pets.",
    "In the Book of Tobit, found in some Bibles, a young man called Tobias sets off on a journey with the angel Raphael, and his dog goes too. It is one of the few friendly dogs in the Bible.",
    "In a story Jesus told in the Gospel of Luke, a poor man called Lazarus lies hungry at a rich man's gate, and the only kindness he receives is from dogs that come and lick his sores.",
    "In the Gospel stories, a woman asks Jesus to heal her daughter, saying that even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table. Jesus praises her faith.",
    "Saint Roch is known as the patron saint of dogs. The story goes that when he fell ill with plague, a dog brought him bread every day and licked his wounds until he recovered.",
    "In medieval France, a greyhound called Guinefort was honoured as a saint by local people, in a story very like that of Gelert. The Church never approved, and tried to stop it.",
    "Saint Hubert is the patron saint of hunters. The St Hubert Hound, bred by monks at his abbey in Belgium, is an ancestor of today's Bloodhound.",
    "The Saint Bernard dog is named after the hospice of Saint Bernard of Menthon, a monk who founded a shelter for travellers high in the Swiss Alps around 1050.",
    "The Dominican friars were nicknamed Domini canes, a Latin pun meaning the hounds of the Lord, because they were seen as guarding the faith like watchdogs.",
    "In some old Orthodox Christian icons, Saint Christopher is painted with the head of a dog, because of ancient stories about a tribe of dog-headed people.",
    "Every year around 4 October, the feast of Saint Francis of Assisi, many churches hold a blessing of the animals, where people bring their dogs to be blessed.",
    "From the 1500s to the 1800s, many English churches paid a dog whipper, whose job was to drive stray dogs out of church during services.",
    "Some old Welsh churches still have dog tongs, long wooden tongs with spikes, used to grab unruly dogs by the neck and remove them from services.",
    "In the Quran, the story of the Companions of the Cave tells of young men who slept for many years in a cave, guarded by their faithful dog, who lay stretched out at the entrance.",
    "In Islamic tradition, the dog of the Companions of the Cave is often called Qitmir, and is said in some traditions to be one of the few animals promised a place in paradise.",
    "A famous saying of the Prophet Muhammad tells of a man who climbed down a well to fetch water for a thirsty dog, and was forgiven his sins for his kindness.",
    "Many Muslims consider dogs unclean, so they may not keep them in the house, but the Quran allows trained dogs to be used for hunting, and dogs are often kept as guards and herders.",
    "In Hindu tradition, Yama, the god of death, has two fierce four-eyed dogs that guard the path to the next world.",
    "In the Hindu epic the Mahabharata, the good king Yudhishthira refuses to enter heaven unless his faithful dog can come too. The dog turns out to be the god Dharma, testing his loyalty.",
    "The Hindu god Bhairava, a fierce form of Shiva, rides a dog, and some temples to Bhairava feed and care for dogs.",
    "The Hindu god Dattatreya is often shown with four dogs at his side, which some say stand for the four holy books called the Vedas.",
    "In the ancient Hindu Rig Veda, Sarama is the divine dog of the god Indra, who tracks down stolen cows for him.",
    "In Nepal, during the festival of Tihar, one day called Kukur Tihar is devoted to dogs. Dogs are given garlands of flowers, a red mark on the forehead and delicious food.",
    "In the ancient Zoroastrian religion of Persia, dogs were deeply respected. Harming a dog was a serious sin, and dogs were fed as a religious duty.",
    "In Zoroastrian funerals, a dog was traditionally brought to look at the body, a ritual called sagdid, meaning seen by a dog, because dogs were thought to drive away evil.",
    "In the Chinese zodiac, the Dog is one of the 12 animals, each ruling a year in turn. People born in a Year of the Dog are said to be loyal and honest.",
    "Myth: The stone animals guarding Chinese temples and palaces are foo dogs. The truth: They are really guardian lions, though they are often called dogs in English because of their doggy look.",
    "At many Japanese Shinto shrines, a pair of stone guardians called komainu, part lion and part dog, stand at the entrance to keep away evil spirits.",
    "The Aztecs of Mexico believed a dog helped the souls of the dead cross a great river on the way to the underworld, Mictlan.",
    "In Norse myth, a monstrous blood-stained hound called Garm guards the gates of Hel, the realm of the dead, and will break free at the end of the world.",
    "In Welsh legend, Cŵn Annwn are the ghostly hounds of the Otherworld, white with red ears. They belong to Arawn, lord of Annwn, and their baying was said to foretell a death.",
    "In British folklore, the Wild Hunt is a ghostly hunting party that races across the sky on stormy nights with a pack of spectral hounds.",
    "In Greek myth, the goddess Hecate, linked with crossroads, magic and the night, was often shown with dogs, and people left food for her at crossroads.",
    "In Greek myth, Laelaps was a magical dog that always caught whatever it chased. When it chased a fox that could never be caught, the god Zeus turned both into stone.",
    "In ancient Egypt, as well as Anubis, there was Wepwawet, a wolf-like god whose name means opener of the ways, who led the way for the king and for the dead.",
    "In ancient Egyptian belief, Anubis helped weigh a dead person's heart against a feather, to decide whether they could enter the afterlife.",
    "The word cynic comes from the Greek for dog-like. The ancient philosopher Diogenes, who lived rough in the streets like a dog, was nicknamed the Dog, and his followers were called Cynics.",
    "In English folklore, a church grim was a guardian spirit, often a black dog, believed to protect a churchyard from evil.",
    "In Yorkshire folklore, the Barghest is a huge black dog with glowing eyes. It was said to roam the streets of towns such as York and Whitby at night.",
    "In the Old Testament, the Book of Exodus says that on the night the Israelites left Egypt, not a dog would bark against them, a sign of God's protection.",
    "In Jewish law in the Bible, meat from an animal killed by wild beasts was not to be eaten, but thrown to the dogs, the Book of Exodus says.",
    "Some Buddhist stories, called Jataka tales, tell of the Buddha in earlier lives, and in some of them he is born as a dog who leads and protects other dogs.",
    "In Tibetan Buddhist monasteries, small dogs such as the Lhasa Apso and Tibetan Spaniel were kept as watchdogs and companions by the monks.",
    "In Christian art, a dog in a painting often stands for loyalty and faithfulness, which is why dogs appear in so many wedding and family portraits.",
    "The name Fido, a popular name for dogs, comes from the Latin fidus, meaning faithful.",
    "In some Christian traditions, Saint Roch is shown in paintings and statues with a small dog at his side carrying a loaf of bread in its mouth.",
    "In the Scottish Highlands, the Cù Sìth was a fairy dog, green and as big as a calf, whose barking was said to be a warning.",
    "In medieval bestiaries, illustrated books about animals that taught religious lessons, the dog was praised for its loyalty, and its tongue was said to heal wounds by licking them.",
    "In Hindu tradition, feeding stray dogs is seen as a good deed, and in many parts of India people leave out food for street dogs every day.",
    "Many religions tell stories of dogs guarding the way to the afterlife, from Egypt's Anubis and Greece's Cerberus to Hinduism's dogs of Yama and Norse Garm, showing how people everywhere trusted dogs at life's biggest door.",
  ],
  "The dog's wild relatives": [
    "The dog belongs to a family of animals called the Canidae, or canids. There are about 37 wild species, including wolves, coyotes, jackals, foxes and wild dogs.",
    "Wild members of the dog family live on every continent except Antarctica. Even Australia has one, the dingo, which arrived with people thousands of years ago.",
    "Most members of the dog family share the same design: long legs for running, a long muzzle, a bushy tail, and they walk on their toes.",
    "Dogs belong to a large group of meat-eating mammals called the Carnivora, which is split into two branches: the dog-like animals and the cat-like animals.",
    "The dog-like branch of meat-eaters includes some surprising cousins of the dog: bears, raccoons, weasels, and even seals, sea lions and walruses.",
    "Myth: Hyenas are a kind of wild dog. The truth: Although they look doggy and hunt in packs, hyenas belong to the cat-like branch of meat-eaters, and are more closely related to cats and mongooses than to dogs.",
    "Myth: Meerkats are related to dogs. The truth: Meerkats are a kind of mongoose, part of the cat-like branch of meat-eaters. They are no closer to dogs than a lion is.",
    "Myth: Raccoons and raccoon dogs are the same animal. The truth: The raccoon dog is a true member of the dog family from East Asia, while the raccoon is a separate animal from the Americas that only looks similar.",
    "Rats, mice and other rodents are not related to dogs at all. They belong to a completely different group of mammals, and many dogs, especially terriers, were bred to catch them.",
    "Myth: The Tasmanian tiger, or thylacine, was a kind of wild dog. The truth: It looked a lot like a dog, but it was a marsupial that carried its babies in a pouch, like a kangaroo. It is believed to have died out in 1936.",
    "The earliest members of the dog family lived in North America around 40 million years ago. One of the first, called Hesperocyon, was a small, long-bodied animal about the size of a fox.",
    "Dogs, wolves, coyotes and golden jackals all belong to the same group, called Canis, and can have puppies together. Foxes cannot breed with dogs, because they are too different.",
    "A pet dog has 78 chromosomes, the tiny packages that carry genes, the same number as a wolf or coyote. A red fox has far fewer, which is one reason dogs and foxes cannot have puppies together.",
    "The coyote lives across North and Central America. Its name comes from coyotl, a word in Nahuatl, the language of the Aztecs.",
    "A coyote is much smaller than a wolf, usually weighing about 9 to 23kg, about the size of a medium dog such as a Border Collie.",
    "Coyotes have moved into big cities such as Chicago and Los Angeles, where they live in parks and gardens, eating rats, mice, fruit and rubbish.",
    "Coyotes are sometimes called song dogs, because of their wild chorus of yips, barks and howls, often heard at dusk.",
    "Coyotes can run at up to about 65km/h, fast enough to catch hares and to escape from most dogs.",
    "When coyotes breed with pet dogs the puppies are called coydogs, and in eastern North America many coyotes carry some wolf DNA, and are nicknamed coywolves.",
    "In many Native American stories, Coyote is a clever trickster, sometimes helpful, often mischievous, who gets into trouble through his own tricks.",
    "The golden jackal lives across southern Asia, the Middle East and south-east Europe, and in recent years it has been spreading north and west through Europe.",
    "Golden jackals have been seen in new countries in Europe in recent years, and scientists think a warmer climate and fewer wolves may be helping them spread.",
    "The black-backed jackal of southern and eastern Africa has a dark saddle of silver and black fur along its back, and a reddish body.",
    "The side-striped jackal of Africa has a pale stripe along each side and a white tip to its tail. It eats more fruit and insects than other jackals.",
    "Jackal pairs often stay together for life, and older pups sometimes stay at home for a year to help their parents raise the next litter.",
    "In 2015, scientists found that the golden jackals of Africa were really a separate species, now called the African golden wolf, more closely related to wolves than to jackals.",
    "The word jackal comes, through Turkish, from shagal, a Persian word for the animal.",
    "The red fox is the most widespread wild meat-eater on Earth. It lives across Europe, Asia, North Africa and North America, and people took it to Australia.",
    "Red foxes were taken to Australia in the 1800s so that settlers could hunt them. They spread across much of the country and have harmed many small native animals.",
    "Foxes started moving into British towns and cities in the 1930s, as new suburbs with big gardens spread. Today urban foxes are a common sight in London.",
    "A female fox is called a vixen, a male is a dog or tod, and the babies are called cubs or kits.",
    "Foxes are famous for their screams. The eerie shriek you may hear on winter nights is usually a vixen calling to a mate.",
    "A fox catches mice under snow or long grass by leaping high and diving nose-first. Some scientists think foxes may use the Earth's magnetic field to aim their pounce.",
    "Foxes often bury spare food to eat later, a habit called caching, and seem to remember where most of their hiding places are.",
    "Unlike most members of the dog family, foxes have slit-shaped pupils, rather like a cat's, which help them see well in dim light.",
    "A red fox's bushy tail is called a brush, and nearly every red fox has a white tip to its tail.",
    "In medieval European stories, a sly, clever fox called Reynard tricks the other animals and gets away with it. In English, a fox is still sometimes called Reynard.",
    "Roald Dahl's Fantastic Mr Fox, from 1970, is about a clever fox who outwits three nasty farmers, Boggis, Bunce and Bean.",
    "Hunting foxes with packs of dogs was banned in England and Wales by the Hunting Act 2004, and in Scotland in 2002. Some hunts now follow a trail of scent instead.",
    "The Arctic fox's coat changes with the seasons: thick and white in winter to hide in the snow, and brown or grey in summer to match the rocks and plants.",
    "The Arctic fox can survive temperatures below minus 50°C, thanks to its thick fur, small ears, short legs, and fur on the soles of its feet.",
    "Arctic foxes sometimes follow polar bears across the ice, to eat the scraps left over from the bears' seal meals.",
    "When it sleeps, an Arctic fox wraps its big, fluffy tail around its body and face like a warm blanket.",
    "A few Arctic foxes have a blue-grey coat all year round, especially in coastal areas. They are called blue foxes, though they are the same species.",
    "The fennec fox of the Sahara Desert is the smallest member of the dog family. An adult weighs only about 1 to 1.5kg, less than a bag of sugar.",
    "The fennec fox has the largest ears for its size of any member of the dog family, up to 15cm long. They help it hear insects underground and give off heat to keep it cool.",
    "The fennec fox has thick fur on the soles of its feet, which protects them from the burning hot desert sand.",
    "The fennec fox can go a long time without drinking, getting most of the water it needs from its food.",
    "The fennec fox is the national animal of Algeria, and the country's football team is nicknamed Les Fennecs.",
    "The grey fox of North America is one of the few members of the dog family that can climb trees. It uses its hooked claws to scramble up trunks to escape danger or find food.",
    "The bat-eared fox of Africa uses its huge ears to listen for termites and beetles, which make up most of its diet.",
    "The bat-eared fox has more teeth than almost any other land mammal with a placenta, up to 48, with lots of small cheek teeth for crunching insects.",
    "The kit fox of North American deserts is one of the smallest foxes, with big ears that help it stay cool in the heat.",
    "Blanford's fox, which lives in the mountains of the Middle East, can climb steep rocky cliffs like a cat, using sharp curved claws and hairless pads.",
    "The Tibetan fox, from the high plateau of Tibet, has a strange square-looking face, which has made it famous in internet pictures.",
    "Darwin's fox lives only in Chile. Charles Darwin collected one on his voyage round the world in 1834, and today it is one of the rarest foxes in the world.",
    "The island fox lives only on six of the Channel Islands off California. It is about the size of a house cat and is descended from grey foxes that reached the islands thousands of years ago.",
    "The crab-eating fox of South America does eat crabs on muddy riverbanks, but it also eats fruit, insects, frogs and small animals.",
    "The raccoon dog comes from East Asia. With its dark mask, it looks like a raccoon, but it is a true member of the dog family.",
    "The raccoon dog is the only member of the dog family known to become sleepy and inactive for long spells in winter, a bit like hibernating.",
    "In Japan, the raccoon dog is called the tanuki. In Japanese folklore it is a cheerful, mischievous shape-shifter, and tanuki statues often stand outside restaurants for luck.",
    "Raccoon dogs were brought to western Russia in the 1900s to be farmed for their fur. Some escaped or were released, and they have since spread across much of Europe.",
    "Raccoon dogs are listed as an invasive species in Europe, because they can harm native wildlife. In Britain it is against the law to release them into the wild, and keeping them is tightly restricted.",
    "The African wild dog is also called the painted dog, because every one has a unique pattern of black, yellow, brown and white patches, like a fingerprint.",
    "The African wild dog's scientific name, Lycaon pictus, means painted wolf.",
    "African wild dogs have only four toes on their front feet. Almost every other member of the dog family, including pet dogs, has five.",
    "In 2017 scientists found that African wild dogs seem to vote on whether to go hunting, by sneezing. The more sneezes in the pack, the more likely they are to set off.",
    "African wild dogs are among the most successful hunters in Africa. Working as a team, they catch their prey far more often than lions do.",
    "African wild dogs look after each other. They share food with pups, and even with old or injured members of the pack that cannot hunt.",
    "African wild dogs are endangered. Only a few thousand are thought to be left in the wild, mainly because of lost habitat, conflict with farmers and diseases caught from dogs.",
    "The dhole, or Asian wild dog, is a reddish wild dog from the forests of Asia. It lives in packs and communicates with strange whistling calls.",
    "Dholes hunt in packs and can bring down deer much bigger than themselves. They are endangered, with only a few thousand left in the wild.",
    "In Rudyard Kipling's The Second Jungle Book, the story Red Dog tells of a huge pack of dholes invading the jungle, and Mowgli leading the fight against them.",
    "The dingo is Australia's wild dog. It arrived around 3,500 years ago with people from Asia, and is now Australia's largest land predator.",
    "Dingoes rarely bark. Instead they howl, and their howls carry across the bush to keep packs in touch.",
    "The Dingo Fence in south-eastern Australia is one of the longest fences in the world, about 5,600km long, built to keep dingoes away from sheep farms.",
    "Dingoes have very flexible joints and can turn their wrists, which helps them climb, and even open some gates and latches.",
    "Unlike pet dogs, which can have puppies twice a year, female dingoes usually breed only once a year.",
    "Scientists still argue about whether the dingo is its own species, a kind of wolf, or a type of dog that went wild long ago.",
    "The New Guinea singing dog is famous for its extraordinary howl, which slides up and down in pitch like a song, unlike any other dog.",
    "For many years the New Guinea singing dog was thought to be extinct in the wild, until studies in the 2010s found wild dogs in the highlands of New Guinea that seem to be the same animals.",
    "The bush dog of South America has short legs, a long body and a short tail, and looks more like a small bear or a weasel than a dog.",
    "Bush dogs have partly webbed feet and are good swimmers. They hunt in packs, sometimes chasing prey into the water.",
    "Bush dogs keep in touch in the thick forest with high-pitched squeaks and whistles, and often hunt at night.",
    "The maned wolf of South America is neither a wolf nor a fox, but a species of its own. It has very long legs, which let it see over tall grass.",
    "The maned wolf eats a lot of fruit, especially a tomato-like fruit called the lobeira, or wolf apple.",
    "The maned wolf has a strong, skunky smell, which it uses to mark its territory.",
    "Brazil's 200 real banknote, introduced in 2020, shows a maned wolf.",
    "The maned wolf lives alone or in pairs, not in packs, and is mostly active at dusk and at night.",
    "The Ethiopian wolf is the rarest member of the dog family in the world. Only about 500 are thought to be left, living high in the mountains of Ethiopia.",
    "The Ethiopian wolf lives mostly on giant mole-rats, rodents that it digs out of their burrows in the high mountain grasslands.",
    "The Ethiopian wolf has a bright red-brown coat with white patches, and a long, narrow muzzle, perfect for grabbing rodents.",
    "The red wolf of the south-eastern United States is one of the most endangered wolves in the world. Only a few dozen live in the wild, in North Carolina.",
    "The dire wolf was a big wolf-like hunter that lived in the Americas during the last Ice Age and died out about 13,000 years ago.",
    "More than 4,000 dire wolves have been found in the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles, where animals got trapped in sticky tar.",
    "A 2021 DNA study found that dire wolves were not close relatives of grey wolves at all, but a separate branch of the dog family.",
    "The Falkland Islands wolf was the only native land mammal on the Falkland Islands. It was so tame it walked up to people, and it died out in 1876.",
    "Charles Darwin visited the Falkland Islands in 1833 and predicted that the islands' tame wolf would soon be wiped out. He was right.",
    "The Japanese wolf, a small wolf that lived in Japan, is believed to have died out in 1905, when the last known one was killed.",
    "The short-eared dog lives in the Amazon rainforest and is so shy and rarely seen that scientists still know very little about it.",
    "Coyote pups are born in a den, usually four to seven at a time. Both parents help raise them, carrying food home in their stomachs and bringing it back up for the pups to eat.",
    "Coyotes and American badgers sometimes hunt together. The badger digs into burrows while the coyote waits outside, and between them they catch more ground squirrels than either would alone.",
    "Coyotes live in every state of the USA except Hawaii, and their range stretches from Alaska down to Panama.",
    "Coyotes spread across much of North America over the last hundred years or so, moving into areas where wolves had been wiped out.",
    "Coyotes will eat almost anything: mice, rabbits, insects, fruit, berries and even rubbish, which helps them survive in cities.",
    "A running coyote usually carries its tail low, pointing down, while a running wolf tends to hold its tail out straight. It is one way to tell them apart.",
    "A coyote family's evening chorus of yips and howls can make just two or three coyotes sound like a whole pack.",
    "Coyote pairs often stay together for years, sharing a territory that they mark with scent and defend from other coyotes.",
    "In recent years scientists moved the black-backed and side-striped jackals into a group of their own, called Lupulella, because DNA showed they are only distant cousins of the golden jackal.",
    "Black-backed jackals sometimes follow lions and hyenas to pick up scraps from their kills, but they are also good hunters of small animals themselves.",
    "Jackal pairs sing duets, howling together to tell other jackals that the territory is taken.",
    "In the old Indian fables of the Panchatantra, jackals are often the clever tricksters of the stories.",
    "In Rudyard Kipling's The Jungle Book, Tabaqui the jackal is the sly, scrounging sidekick of the tiger Shere Khan.",
    "Golden jackals eat almost anything they can find, from small animals and birds to fruit, and even grapes from vineyards.",
    "A fox's home is called an earth or a den. Foxes often move into old rabbit burrows or badger setts rather than digging their own.",
    "Fox cubs are usually born in March or April, blind and covered in dark brown fur. Their coats turn red as they grow.",
    "Red foxes are not always red. Some are black with silver-tipped fur, called silver foxes, and others have a dark cross across their shoulders, called cross foxes. All are the same species.",
    "Foxes eat a surprising number of earthworms. On damp nights they can be seen in fields and lawns, pulling worms out of the ground.",
    "A red fox can run at up to about 50km/h, and can leap fences as tall as a grown-up.",
    "Wild red foxes usually live only two or three years, although in zoos they can live more than ten.",
    "Foxes make many sounds besides screaming, including a chattering noise called gekkering, which they make when playing or squabbling.",
    "The red fox's scientific name is Vulpes vulpes. Vulpes is the Latin word for fox.",
    "Foxes are famous for stealing things. Urban foxes have been known to carry off shoes, gloves, toys and even golf balls from golf courses.",
    "The red fox is the only wild member of the dog family living in Britain today. Wolves once lived here too, but were wiped out hundreds of years ago.",
    "In 2019 scientists reported that a young Arctic fox had walked about 3,500km, from Norway's Svalbard islands across the sea ice to Canada, in just 76 days.",
    "When there are plenty of lemmings to eat, Arctic foxes can have huge litters, sometimes more than 20 cubs, one of the largest litters of any meat-eating mammal.",
    "Arctic fox numbers rise and fall with the lemmings, the small rodents they mainly eat. In good lemming years, many more fox cubs survive.",
    "The Arctic fox is so well insulated that it is said not to start shivering until the temperature drops to around minus 70°C.",
    "The Arctic fox was the only land mammal living in Iceland when the first people arrived there, more than 1,000 years ago.",
    "Fennec foxes sleep through the hot desert day in underground dens, and come out at night to hunt insects, lizards and small rodents.",
    "Fennec foxes dig dens in sand dunes, sometimes with several tunnels and entrances, which can be shared by a family.",
    "A fennec fox can leap about 60cm straight up, and more than a metre forwards, to pounce on prey.",
    "The name fennec comes from fanak, an Arabic word for fox.",
    "The bat-eared fox can hear termites and beetle larvae moving underground, then digs them out with its front paws.",
    "Insects, especially termites and beetles, make up most of the bat-eared fox's food. It rarely hunts larger animals.",
    "The grey fox and its close relative, the island fox, belong to one of the oldest branches of the dog family still alive today.",
    "The raccoon dog's scientific name, Nyctereutes, means night wanderer, because it mostly comes out after dark.",
    "Raccoon dogs eat frogs, fish, insects, berries and fruit, and often search for food along rivers and lakes.",
    "Raccoon dog pairs usually stay together, and the father helps look after the pups, bringing food and guarding the den.",
    "African wild dogs live in packs, usually of about 6 to 20 adults. In most packs only one pair has pups, and the whole pack helps raise them.",
    "When African wild dogs come back from a hunt, the pups are allowed to eat first, before even the pack leaders.",
    "African wild dogs have huge, rounded ears, which help them hear each other and also help them lose heat in the African sun.",
    "African wild dogs can run at around 60km/h and keep up a chase for several kilometres, wearing their prey out rather than ambushing it.",
    "A pack of African wild dogs roams across a huge area, often hundreds of square kilometres, so they need wide open spaces to survive.",
    "In African wild dog packs, young females usually leave home to find a new pack, while young males often stay with the pack they were born into.",
    "Dholes keep in touch in the thick forest with high whistling calls, which is why they are sometimes called whistling dogs.",
    "A pack of dholes shares its food, and the whole pack helps feed and protect the pups, bringing back meat for them.",
    "The dhole has a rusty red coat and a bushy black-tipped tail, and lives in forests from India to South-East Asia.",
    "The word dingo comes from the Dharug language of the Aboriginal people of the Sydney area.",
    "Most dingoes are a sandy ginger colour, often with white feet and a white tail tip, though some are black with tan markings, and a few are white.",
    "The dingoes of K'gari, an island off Queensland once called Fraser Island, are thought to be among the purest dingoes in Australia, with little dog ancestry.",
    "Dingoes can bark, but they rarely do. Their main calls are howls, moans and whines.",
    "New Guinea singing dogs are very agile and can climb trees, and their eyes glow a bright green in the dark.",
    "Bush dogs often live in burrows dug by other animals, such as armadillos, in the forests and grasslands of South America.",
    "Female bush dogs mark their territory in a funny way: they do a handstand on their front legs to leave their scent as high up as possible.",
    "The maned wolf's very long legs make it look as if it is on stilts. It is not built for speed, and hunts by listening, then pouncing, like a fox.",
    "By eating the wolf apple fruit and leaving the seeds in its droppings, the maned wolf helps spread the plant across the grasslands of South America.",
    "The maned wolf gets its name from the mane of black fur on its neck and shoulders, which stands up when it feels threatened.",
    "The maned wolf eats small mammals, birds and insects as well as fruit, so its diet is about half meat and half plants.",
    "Ethiopian wolves live in family packs, but each wolf usually hunts alone, patiently stalking rodents across the high grasslands.",
    "Ethiopian wolves live high in the mountains, mostly above 3,000 metres, where the air is thin and cold.",
    "In 2024 scientists reported that Ethiopian wolves lick sweet nectar from the flowers of the Ethiopian red hot poker plant, the first large meat-eater known to feed on nectar.",
    "One of the biggest dangers to Ethiopian wolves is rabies caught from village dogs, so conservation teams vaccinate the wolves to protect them.",
    "The red wolf was declared extinct in the wild in 1980. The last few had been caught and bred in captivity, and in 1987 red wolves were released back into the wild in North Carolina.",
    "Every red wolf alive today descends from just 14 wolves that were caught in the 1970s to save the species.",
    "Scientists still debate the red wolf's history. Some think it is a separate species, and others think it came from long-ago mixing between grey wolves and coyotes.",
    "The dire wolf was heavier than most grey wolves, with a bigger head and a stronger bite, well suited to hunting large Ice Age animals such as horses and bison.",
    "In 2025 an American company, Colossal Biosciences, announced wolf pups it called dire wolves, made by changing some genes in grey wolf cells. Many scientists said they were really modified grey wolves, not true dire wolves.",
    "Dire wolves became famous around the world through the television series Game of Thrones, where the Stark children each raise a dire wolf pup.",
    "Millions of years ago, North America was home to bone-crushing dogs called borophagines, with powerful jaws for cracking bones, a bit like hyenas today.",
    "The largest member of the dog family ever known was Epicyon, a bone-crushing dog that lived in North America millions of years ago and may have weighed as much as a small bear.",
    "The culpeo, or Andean fox, is the second-largest member of the dog family in South America, after the maned wolf. It lives in the Andes mountains.",
    "The swift fox of the North American prairies is one of the fastest small foxes, and can run at around 50km/h.",
    "The swift fox disappeared from Canada in the 1900s, but was brought back in the 1980s by releasing foxes bred in captivity, and has been recovering since.",
    "The island fox of California nearly died out in the 1990s, mainly because of golden eagles. Thanks to a rescue plan it recovered so quickly that in 2016 it was taken off the endangered list.",
    "The corsac fox lives on the dry grasslands of Central Asia and, unlike most foxes, often lives in small groups and shares dens.",
    "The Bengal fox of India has a black tip to its tail, unlike the white-tipped tail of the red fox.",
    "Rüppell's fox lives in the deserts of North Africa and the Middle East, and, like the fennec, has big ears and furry feet for life on hot sand.",
    "The pale fox lives along the southern edge of the Sahara Desert and is one of the least studied foxes in the world.",
    "The Cape fox is the only true fox living in southern Africa. It has silvery-grey fur and a big bushy tail.",
    "The hoary fox of Brazil is small and eats mostly termites, much like the bat-eared fox of Africa, though the two are not closely related.",
    "In most members of the dog family, fathers help raise the young. Pairing up and sharing the work of feeding the pups is unusual among mammals, but common in canids.",
    "Many members of the dog family feed their pups by bringing food back in their stomach and bringing it up for the pups, a habit pet puppies still show when they lick an adult's mouth.",
    "All members of the dog family have special cheek teeth called carnassials, which work like scissors to slice meat.",
    "Unlike cats, most members of the dog family cannot pull their claws back in. That gives them grip when running, but makes climbing harder.",
    "All members of the dog family mark their territory with scent, using urine, droppings and glands, a message system that other animals can read like a noticeboard.",
    "Most wild members of the dog family are most active at dawn, dusk and night, when it is cooler and their prey is on the move.",
    "The smallest member of the dog family is the fennec fox, at about 1 to 1.5kg. The largest wild one is the grey wolf, with big males weighing over 50kg.",
    "The dog family first evolved in North America around 40 million years ago, then spread to Asia, Europe, Africa and finally South America.",
    "Members of the dog family reached South America only about 3 million years ago, when a land bridge formed between North and South America. Since then many unusual species, like the bush dog and maned wolf, evolved there.",
    "Dogs, wolves, coyotes, jackals, dholes and African wild dogs belong to the wolf-like branch of the dog family. Foxes form a separate branch.",
    "Myth: Foxes are a kind of cat, because of their slit pupils and pouncing. The truth: Foxes are full members of the dog family. They simply evolved some cat-like tricks for hunting small prey.",
    "Myth: Wild dogs are just pet dogs that ran away. The truth: The African wild dog, the dhole and the bush dog are separate wild species that have never been tamed.",
    "A dog that has gone wild is called feral. Dingoes are sometimes described this way, because they are thought to come from tame dogs that went wild long ago.",
    "Some members of the dog family are threatened by diseases caught from pet dogs, such as rabies and distemper, which is why vaccinating village dogs helps protect wildlife.",
    "Many wild members of the dog family are losing their homes as forests and grasslands are turned into farms and towns, one of the biggest threats they face.",
    "Scientists study wild members of the dog family using camera traps, cameras that take a picture whenever an animal walks past, even in the middle of the night.",
    "Some rare members of the dog family, such as the Ethiopian wolf and the African wild dog, are tracked with radio or GPS collars, so scientists can follow where they travel.",
    "Studying the dog's wild relatives helps scientists understand how our pet dogs came to be, and which of their habits, like digging, howling and burying food, are very old.",
    "Pet dogs still share many habits with their wild relatives: burying bones, turning in circles before lying down, rolling in smelly things and howling along with sirens.",
    "The Arctic wolf, a white grey wolf from the far north of Canada and Greenland, lives where it is dark for months in winter and hunts musk oxen and Arctic hares.",
    "The Mexican wolf, or lobo, is the smallest and rarest kind of grey wolf in North America. It had vanished from the wild, but was released back into Arizona and New Mexico in 1998.",
    "The Arabian wolf is one of the smallest wolves in the world, weighing only about 18kg, and has big ears to help it lose heat in the desert.",
    "The wolves of the Himalayas, living high in the mountains of Nepal and Tibet, carry very old DNA and may be one of the oldest wolf families still alive.",
    "The Indian wolf lives on the grasslands and scrublands of India, and is smaller and lighter than the wolves of Europe and North America.",
    "Wolves in Italy nearly died out in the 1970s, when only about 100 were left in the Apennine mountains. After they were protected, they recovered and have spread back across much of the country.",
    "Wolves have been returning to parts of Europe where they had long been wiped out. They came back to Germany around 2000, and to Denmark in 2012, for the first time in about 200 years.",
    "The Iberian wolf of Spain and Portugal has dark marks on its front legs and a white patch on its upper lip.",
    "Northern Inuit dogs, a British type of dog bred to look like wolves, played the dire wolf pups in the television series Game of Thrones.",
    "Grey wolves do not all look grey. Their coats can be white, black, brown, cream or mixed, depending on where they live.",
    "The ancestor of both foxes and wolves may have been a small animal called Leptocyon, which lived in North America millions of years ago.",
    "In 2021, scientists gave the dire wolf a new scientific name, Aenocyon dirus, meaning terrible wolf, after DNA showed it was not a close relative of the grey wolf.",
    "On the islands of Sardinia and Corsica there once lived a small wild dog called the Sardinian dhole, which died out thousands of years ago.",
    "A fox-like animal related to the Falklands wolf, called Dusicyon avus, lived in South America until only a few hundred years ago.",
    "The word canine means to do with dogs, from the Latin canis, meaning dog. Vulpine means fox-like, from vulpes, and lupine means wolf-like, from lupus.",
    "The study of dogs, their breeds and their history is called cynology, from the Greek word for dog.",
    "Crying wolf means raising a false alarm. It comes from Aesop's fable of the shepherd boy who tricked the villagers so often with false wolf alarms that nobody believed him when a real wolf came.",
    "A wolf in sheep's clothing is someone who seems friendly but is really dangerous. The idea appears in the Bible and in old fables.",
    "To keep the wolf from the door means to have just enough money to avoid going hungry, as if hunger were a wolf waiting outside.",
    "To wolf down your food means to eat it very fast and greedily, the way a hungry wolf gulps its meal.",
    "A lone wolf is a person who likes to do things on their own. Real wolves usually live in packs, but young wolves sometimes leave to live alone for a while.",
    "Sly as a fox describes someone clever and cunning, because of the fox's reputation in stories for tricking other animals.",
    "Letting the fox guard the henhouse means putting someone in charge of the very thing they are most likely to steal or spoil.",
    "An old book with brown spots on its pages is said to be foxed, perhaps because the spots are the reddish-brown colour of a fox.",
    "To outfox someone means to beat them by being even more cunning, like outwitting a fox.",
    "In Aesop's fable The Fox and the Grapes, a fox that cannot reach some grapes decides they must be sour anyway. That is where the saying sour grapes comes from.",
    "In Aesop's fable The Fox and the Crow, a fox flatters a crow into singing, so it drops the cheese in its beak, a warning about trusting flattery.",
    "In Scotland and northern England, an old name for a fox is a tod.",
    "In Britain, foxes are sometimes nicknamed Charlie, after the 18th-century politician Charles James Fox.",
    "Leicester City Football Club is nicknamed the Foxes, and has a fox on its badge. The team famously won the Premier League in 2016.",
    "Basil Brush, a cheeky fox puppet famous for his laugh and his catchphrase Boom Boom, has been on British television since the 1960s.",
    "In Beatrix Potter's The Tale of Mr. Tod, from 1912, Mr Tod is a bad-tempered fox who battles a badger called Tommy Brock.",
    "In Beatrix Potter's The Tale of Jemima Puddle-Duck, the foxy-whiskered gentleman who invites Jemima to lay her eggs at his house is really a hungry fox.",
    "In 2011, a fox was found living on the 72nd floor of the Shard in London while the skyscraper was still being built. The builders nicknamed him Romeo.",
    "Red foxes have a scent gland on the top of the tail, sometimes called the violet gland, which gives off a musky smell.",
    "A fox's footprint is narrower and neater than a dog's, and a fox walks in a straight line, placing its back feet in the prints of its front feet.",
    "Scientists at the University of Bristol have studied the city's urban foxes since the 1970s, learning much of what we know about how foxes live in towns.",
    "In some fox families, young vixens from the year before stay at home and help their mother raise the new cubs, instead of having cubs of their own.",
    "The number of red foxes in Britain has been estimated at around 350,000, living in the countryside and in towns.",
    "Fox cubs play a lot: pouncing, wrestling and chasing each other outside the den, which helps them learn to hunt.",
    "In Japanese folklore, the kitsune is a magical fox that grows more tails as it gets older and wiser. The most powerful have nine tails.",
    "Foxes are the messengers of Inari, a Japanese Shinto god of rice and good harvests, and stone fox statues guard thousands of Inari shrines in Japan.",
    "In Korean folklore, the kumiho is a nine-tailed fox spirit that can change its shape, a story related to the Chinese and Japanese fox legends.",
    "In Aztec mythology, Huehuecoyotl, meaning old coyote, was a trickster god of music, dance and storytelling.",
    "The BBC series Dynasties, narrated by David Attenborough in 2018, included an episode following a pack of African wild dogs, called painted wolves, in Zimbabwe.",
    "The Ethiopian wolf was once called the Simien fox or Simien jackal, until scientists showed it was really a kind of wolf.",
    "The Bale Mountains in Ethiopia are the stronghold of the Ethiopian wolf, home to more of these rare wolves than anywhere else.",
    "In Brazil, the maned wolf is called the guará, and its long legs have earned it the nickname fox on stilts.",
    "The bush dog is sometimes called the vinegar dog, though nobody is quite sure why.",
    "The grey fox can turn its front legs more than most canids, which, with its hooked claws, lets it hug tree trunks and climb.",
    "Dingoes have lived alongside Aboriginal people for thousands of years, and appear in many of their stories. Some dingoes were kept at camps as companions and hunting partners.",
    "The African wild dog is one of the world's most endangered large meat-eaters, and the Okavango Delta in Botswana is one of its last strongholds.",
    "Coyotes are very adaptable parents. When coyotes are hunted, they often have more pups, which helps their numbers bounce back.",
    "Coyotes have been seen crossing busy roads at crossings and waiting for gaps in traffic, showing how well some have learned to live in cities.",
    "The red fox is listed as one of the world's worst invasive species in Australia, where it has helped push some small native animals to extinction.",
    "In the Arctic, where there are few trees, Arctic foxes dig dens that can be used by generations of foxes for hundreds of years.",
    "The fennec fox's pale sandy coat reflects sunlight during the day and keeps it warm during cold desert nights.",
    "Unlike most foxes, the bat-eared fox often lives in family groups, and the father does much of the babysitting.",
    "The kit fox and the swift fox are such close relatives that they sometimes interbreed where their ranges meet.",
    "The dhole was once found across much of Asia and even into Europe during the Ice Age. Today it survives only in scattered forests in southern and South-East Asia.",
    "New Guinea singing dogs are kept in zoos around the world, where keepers love to hear their unusual howling, which can sound like whale song.",
    "Dingoes are important for Australia's wildlife, because they help keep numbers of kangaroos, and of foxes and feral cats, in check.",
    "Some wild members of the dog family, like wolves and coyotes, can learn to recognise danger from humans and change when they come out, becoming more active at night near towns.",
    "Foxes, wolves and coyotes all mark their territory by scratching the ground, which leaves both a visible sign and scent from glands in their feet.",
    "The African wild dog, the dhole and the bush dog all hunt in packs and share food, even though they live on different continents. Scientists think they came up with the same way of living separately.",
    "Members of the dog family that live in hot places, like the fennec fox, jackals and African wild dog, often have big ears to help them lose heat.",
    "Members of the dog family that live in very cold places, like the Arctic fox, often have small ears, short legs and thick fur, to hold on to heat.",
    "Many members of the dog family keep in touch with howls. Wolves, coyotes, jackals and dingoes howl, while foxes bark and scream instead.",
    "Most members of the dog family have litters of several pups, which are born blind and helpless and stay in a den for the first weeks of life.",
    "In many members of the dog family, older brothers and sisters help look after the new pups, fetching food and babysitting while the parents hunt.",
    "Most wild members of the dog family live only a few years in the wild, but can live much longer in zoos, where they are safe from danger and illness.",
    "Jackals are sometimes seen following big cats to feed on leftovers, but they also chase other scavengers away from food, standing up to animals much bigger than themselves.",
    "Golden jackals have been found living near towns and villages in India for thousands of years, eating scraps and catching rats.",
    "The Sechuran fox, a small fox from the coastal deserts of Peru and Ecuador, eats a lot of seeds and fruit, and helps spread desert plants.",
    "Red foxes in Britain have their cubs in spring, and by autumn the young foxes leave home to find territories of their own, which is when many are seen on roads.",
    "Some towns have many more foxes per square kilometre than the countryside, because gardens and bins provide plenty of food.",
    "Fox cubs are sometimes found alone and thought to be abandoned, but wildlife experts advise watching from a distance first, as the mother is usually nearby.",
    "Wildlife hospitals in Britain care for thousands of injured or orphaned foxes every year, and release them back into the wild when they are well.",
    "Foxes can catch a skin disease called mange, caused by tiny mites, which makes their fur fall out. In the 1990s it greatly reduced the number of foxes in Bristol.",
    "Arctic foxes have been seen travelling far out onto sea ice in winter, scavenging on the remains of seals killed by polar bears.",
    "The coyote was nicknamed the prairie wolf by early European settlers in North America.",
    "In some parts of North America, coyotes and wolves compete for the same prey. Where wolves came back, as in Yellowstone National Park, coyote numbers fell.",
    "The dhole's scientific name, Cuon alpinus, means mountain dog, though it lives in lowland forests as well as mountains.",
    "The African wild dog's Latin name, Lycaon pictus, links it to Lycaon, a king in Greek myth who was turned into a wolf.",
    "The maned wolf's scientific name, Chrysocyon brachyurus, means golden dog with a short tail.",
    "The raccoon dog is common in Finland and other parts of northern Europe, far from its original home in East Asia.",
    "Island foxes are so small because animals that live on islands with little food often become smaller over many generations, a pattern called island dwarfism.",
    "In the Falkland Islands, the Falklands wolf is remembered on stamps and in the islands' museum, as the only native land mammal the islands ever had.",
    "Some scientists think the Falklands wolf's ancestors reached the islands across ice during the last Ice Age, when sea levels were much lower.",
    "The Japanese wolf appears in old Japanese folklore as a protector of farmers, because it hunted the deer and wild boar that damaged crops.",
    "The Ethiopian wolf's long, narrow muzzle is perfect for reaching into burrows and grabbing mole-rats, its favourite food.",
    "The red wolf is smaller than a grey wolf but bigger than a coyote, and has a reddish tinge to its coat, especially behind its ears.",
    "In 2018, scientists found that some coyote-like animals on Galveston Island in Texas carried red wolf DNA, surprising experts who thought the red wolf survived only in North Carolina.",
    "Bush dogs have been seen hunting animals much bigger than themselves, such as capybaras, the world's largest rodents, by working as a team.",
    "Dingoes on the island of K'gari are a famous sight for visitors, who are warned never to feed them, as it makes them bolder around people.",
    "African wild dogs have been reintroduced to some parts of Africa where they had died out, such as parts of South Africa and Mozambique, by moving whole packs to new areas.",
    "Dholes are sometimes called red dogs, and in parts of India they are said to have chased even tigers away from a kill.",
    "The Tibetan fox lives on the high Tibetan plateau, where it often follows brown bears digging for pikas, catching any that escape.",
    "The pampas fox of South America's grasslands will play dead when threatened, lying still until the danger goes away.",
    "From the tiny fennec fox of the Sahara to the huge grey wolf of the Arctic, the members of the dog family have spread to almost every kind of land habitat on Earth.",
  ],
  "Record-breaking dogs": [
    "The oldest dog on record is Bluey, an Australian Cattle Dog from Victoria, Australia, who worked with cattle and sheep for almost 20 years and lived to 29 years and 5 months. He died in 1939.",
    "In 2023, Guinness World Records named a Portuguese dog called Bobi the oldest dog ever, at over 30. In 2024 it withdrew the title, because there was not enough proof of his age.",
    "The tallest dog ever recorded was Zeus, a Great Dane from Michigan in the USA, who measured 111.8cm from paw to shoulder. On his hind legs he stood over 2 metres tall.",
    "In 2022, another Great Dane called Zeus, from Texas, was named the world's tallest living dog, at 104.6cm to the shoulder. Two different record-holding Great Danes were both called Zeus.",
    "Mochi, a Saint Bernard from South Dakota in the USA, set the record for the longest tongue on a dog in 2016. It measured 18.58cm.",
    "Tigger, a Bloodhound from Illinois in the USA, held the record for the longest ears on a dog. His right ear was 34.9cm long and his left 31.7cm.",
    "Keon, a Great Dane, set the record for the longest tail on a dog in 2015. It measured 76.8cm, longer than many small dogs are tall.",
    "The fastest recorded speed for a dog is about 67km/h, set by a Greyhound called Star Title in Australia in 1994.",
    "The record for the highest jump by a dog is 191.7cm, set by a Greyhound called Feather in Maryland, USA, in 2017. That is taller than most grown-ups.",
    "Augie, a Golden Retriever from Texas, set a world record in 2003 by holding five tennis balls in his mouth at once.",
    "Charlie, a Golden Retriever from Adelaide in Australia, set the record for the loudest bark in 2012, at 113.1 decibels, about as loud as a rock concert.",
    "The biggest litter of puppies on record is 24, born to Tia, a Neapolitan Mastiff, in 2004 in Manea, Cambridgeshire.",
    "Otto, an English Bulldog, skateboarded through a tunnel made by the legs of 30 people in Lima, Peru, in 2015, setting a world record.",
    "In 2011, a red Tibetan Mastiff called Big Splash was reportedly sold in China for about 10 million yuan, over £1 million, making him one of the most expensive dogs ever.",
    "Twinkie, a Jack Russell Terrier from California, set a record in 2008 by popping 100 balloons in just 39.08 seconds.",
    "Abbie Girl, a Kelpie cross, set the record for the longest wave surfed by a dog in 2011, riding a wave for 107.2 metres in San Diego, California.",
    "Purin, a Beagle from Japan, set a world record in 2015 by catching 14 balls with her front paws in one minute.",
    "Jiff, a Pomeranian, set records in 2014 for the fastest 10 metres on his hind legs, in 6.56 seconds, and the fastest 5 metres on his front paws, in 7.76 seconds.",
    "Striker, a Border Collie from Canada, set a record in 2004 for the fastest car window wound down by a dog, in 11.34 seconds, using his nose and paw.",
    "Norman, a Briard, set the record for the fastest 30 metres on a scooter by a dog in 2013, in 20.77 seconds.",
    "Midge, a Chihuahua and Rat Terrier cross from Ohio in the USA, was named the world's smallest police dog in 2006. She was trained to sniff out drugs.",
    "In 1991, Crufts was named the world's largest dog show by Guinness World Records, with 22,973 dogs entered.",
    "Owney, a scruffy terrier, rode the mail trains of the USA from 1888 to 1897 and travelled about 140,000 miles, becoming the unofficial mascot of the US Post Office.",
    "The Chihuahua holds the record as the smallest dog breed, and the Irish Wolfhound and Great Dane compete for the title of tallest.",
    "Guinness World Records has a whole category of dog records, from the fastest dogs and the loudest barks to dogs that skateboard, surf and ride scooters.",
    "Bluey's record as the oldest dog lasted for more than 80 years, from 1939 until another claim was made in 2023.",
    "In 2023, a Chihuahua called Spike from Ohio in the USA was named the world's oldest living dog, at 23 years old.",
    "Many dog world records are set by Border Collies, Jack Russells and other clever, energetic breeds that love learning tricks.",
  ],
  "Life-saving and hero dogs": [
    "Swansea Jack, a black retriever, lived by the docks in Swansea in the 1930s and is said to have rescued 27 people from drowning. In 1936 he was named Bravest Dog of the Year.",
    "Swansea Jack has a memorial on the promenade in Swansea, and he is still remembered in the city as one of Britain's most famous hero dogs.",
    "In 1919 the ship SS Ethie was wrecked off Newfoundland. A Newfoundland dog called Tang is said to have swum out with a rope to help bring all 92 people safely to shore.",
    "On 11 September 2001, a guide dog called Roselle led her blind owner, Michael Hingson, down 78 floors of the World Trade Center in New York to safety.",
    "Guide dogs Salty and Roselle were jointly awarded the Dickin Medal in 2002 for guiding their blind owners out of the World Trade Center on 11 September 2001.",
    "Kabang, a dog in the Philippines, jumped in front of a speeding motorbike in 2011 to save two young girls. She lost her snout, but vets in the USA helped her recover.",
    "In 2006, a Beagle called Belle saved her owner, who was having a diabetic emergency, by biting down on his mobile phone to call for help. She won an award for it.",
    "In 2007, a Doberman called Khan in Queensland, Australia, grabbed a toddler and pulled her away from a deadly king brown snake. Khan was bitten, but survived.",
    "In 2018, Max, an old, deaf and partly blind cattle dog, stayed all night with a lost three-year-old girl in the Australian bush until rescuers found her. Police made him an honorary police dog.",
    "In 2016, a police dog called Finn was stabbed while protecting his handler in Stevenage. He recovered, and his case led to Finn's Law in 2019, which gives service animals more protection.",
    "Bretagne, a Golden Retriever, searched the ruins of the World Trade Center after 11 September 2001. When she died in 2016, she was believed to be the last surviving 9/11 search dog.",
    "Frida, a Labrador with the Mexican Navy, became famous in 2017 when she searched for survivors after an earthquake. Over her career she was credited with finding dozens of people.",
    "Trakr, a German Shepherd police dog from Canada, found the last survivor pulled from the rubble of the World Trade Center in 2001.",
    "Diesel, a Belgian Malinois police dog, was killed during a police operation in France in 2015. The PDSA awarded her its Gold Medal for bravery.",
    "In Italy, specially trained water rescue dogs, often Newfoundlands and Labradors, can leap from helicopters into the sea to help swimmers in trouble.",
    "In 2008, a Rottweiler called Eve pulled her disabled owner out of a burning truck in Missouri, USA, just before it burst into flames.",
    "In 2008, a German Shepherd called Buddy called the emergency services by pressing a button on the phone when his owner had a seizure. The call recording made the news worldwide.",
    "In 2010, a Golden Retriever called Angel saved an 11-year-old boy, Austin, from a cougar that attacked him in Canada, by placing herself between them.",
    "In 2007 a Golden Retriever called Toby was reported to have saved his owner from choking by jumping on her chest until the apple she had choked on came loose.",
    "In 1966, a Collie called Pickles found the stolen football World Cup trophy wrapped in newspaper under a hedge in south London, a week before the tournament began.",
    "Pickles became a national hero after finding the World Cup trophy. He was invited to the celebrations after England won, and starred in a film.",
    "The PDSA Gold Medal is given to animals for bravery and devotion to duty in civilian life, such as police dogs, search dogs and pets that save lives.",
    "Hero dogs in Britain can be honoured with the PDSA Gold Medal, the Dickin Medal for animals serving with the armed forces or emergency services, and the PDSA Order of Merit for devotion to their owners.",
    "Many hero dogs are ordinary pets, not trained rescuers. They save lives simply by noticing that something is wrong and refusing to leave their person.",
    "At the Great St Bernard Hospice in the Swiss Alps, it became a tradition to name one Saint Bernard puppy in each generation Barry, after the famous rescue dog.",
    "Hero dogs are honoured every year at awards such as the PDSA Animal Awards and Crufts' Friends for Life competition, which celebrate dogs that have changed or saved lives.",
  ],
  "Loyal dogs": [
    "Hachiko, an Akita in Japan, met his owner, Professor Ueno, at Shibuya station every day. After the professor died suddenly in 1925, Hachiko kept waiting there every day for about nine years.",
    "A bronze statue of Hachiko was put up at Shibuya station in 1934, and the loyal Akita was there to see it. Today it is one of the most famous meeting places in Tokyo.",
    "In 2015, a new statue was put up at the University of Tokyo, showing Hachiko finally being reunited with Professor Ueno, jumping up to greet him.",
    "Hachiko's story was made into a Japanese film in 1987 and an American film, Hachi: A Dog's Tale, in 2009.",
    "Fido was a dog in Italy whose owner, Carlo Soriani, was killed in a bombing raid in 1943. For 14 years, Fido went to the bus stop every evening to wait for him. A statue of Fido stands in his town.",
    "Capitán, a dog in Argentina, is said to have stayed by his owner's grave for about ten years after his owner died in 2006.",
    "Ruswarp, a Border Collie, stayed beside the body of his owner, Graham Nuttall, in the Welsh mountains for 11 weeks in 1990 until they were found. Ruswarp was very weak, but survived.",
    "Ruswarp's paw print was on a petition that helped save the Settle to Carlisle railway line, and a statue of him now stands at Garsdale station in Cumbria.",
    "In 1953, a sheepdog called Tip stayed beside her dead owner, Joseph Tagg, on the Derbyshire moors for 15 weeks. A memorial to her loyalty stands at Derwent Dam.",
    "Old Shep was a dog in Montana, USA, who watched his owner's coffin put on a train in 1936, then met every train at Fort Benton station for over five years, waiting for him to return.",
    "In 1923, a Collie cross called Bobbie was lost on a family trip in Indiana, USA. Six months later he turned up at home in Oregon, having travelled about 4,000km.",
    "In 2011, a Labrador called Hawkeye lay beside the coffin of his owner, a US Navy SEAL, throughout the funeral. The photo was seen by millions of people.",
    "In Togliatti, Russia, a dog called Konstantin is said to have waited for seven years at the roadside where his owners died in an accident. The Monument to Devotion was put up in his honour in 2003.",
    "Station Jim was a dog who collected money at Slough railway station in the 1890s for the children and widows of railway workers. After he died, he was preserved and put on display at the station.",
    "Nipper, a terrier from Bristol, was painted by the artist Francis Barraud listening to a gramophone. The picture, His Master's Voice, became the famous HMV logo.",
    "Jock of the Bushveld is a true story, published in 1907 by Percy FitzPatrick, about his loyal Staffordshire Bull Terrier cross in South Africa in the 1880s.",
    "Stories of dogs waiting faithfully for owners who never come home have been told in Britain, Japan, Italy, Argentina and Russia, showing how loyalty is recognised everywhere.",
    "After Hachiko died in 1935, his body was preserved and can still be seen at the National Science Museum in Tokyo. Some of his remains were buried beside Professor Ueno.",
    "In San Francisco in the 1860s, two stray dogs, Bummer and Lazarus, became so famous for catching rats together that the city let them roam free when other strays were rounded up.",
    "The Italian dog Fido became so famous for waiting at the bus stop that a statue was put up in his town in 1957, while he was still alive.",
    "Hachiko's statue at Shibuya had to be melted down for metal during the Second World War. A new statue was made in 1948, and it still stands there today.",
    "Stories of loyal dogs have inspired films and books around the world, and statues of faithful dogs stand in Edinburgh, Tokyo, Italy, Argentina and Russia.",
  ],
  "War dog stories": [
    "Judy, a Pointer, served on Royal Navy ships in the Second World War and was captured by the Japanese. She was officially registered as a prisoner of war, the only dog known to have been.",
    "In the prison camp, Judy's friend, the airman Frank Williams, shared his tiny rice ration with her. She was awarded the Dickin Medal in 1946.",
    "Antis, a German Shepherd, flew on bombing missions with his owner, a Czech airman in the Royal Air Force, during the Second World War. He won the Dickin Medal in 1949.",
    "Rob, a Collie, is said to have made parachute jumps with the Special Air Service during the Second World War. He was awarded the Dickin Medal in 1945.",
    "Beauty, a wire-haired terrier, dug out 63 animals trapped under bombed buildings during the London Blitz. She was given a silver-mounted collar and the Freedom of Holland Park.",
    "Just Nuisance, a Great Dane, was officially enlisted in the Royal Navy in South Africa in 1939 as an Able Seaman, so that he could ride trains for free with the sailors he followed.",
    "Just Nuisance looked after drunken sailors and walked them back to their ships. When he died in 1944 he was buried with full naval honours, and a statue of him stands in Simon's Town.",
    "Gander, a Newfoundland, served with Canadian soldiers in Hong Kong in 1941. He picked up a grenade thrown at wounded soldiers and ran off with it, losing his life. He won the Dickin Medal in 2000.",
    "Bamse, a huge Saint Bernard, was the mascot of a Norwegian navy ship based in Montrose, Scotland, during the Second World War. A statue of him stands on the harbour in Montrose.",
    "Sheila, a Collie, helped her shepherd find American airmen whose plane crashed in the snowy Cheviot Hills in 1944. She was the first civilian dog to win the Dickin Medal.",
    "Chips, a German Shepherd cross with the US Army, attacked an enemy machine-gun post in Sicily in 1943, forcing the soldiers inside to surrender.",
    "Treo, a black Labrador, found hidden roadside bombs in Afghanistan in 2008, saving many soldiers. He was awarded the Dickin Medal in 2010.",
    "Sadie, a Labrador, found a hidden bomb outside a United Nations base in Kabul, Afghanistan, in 2005. She won the Dickin Medal in 2007.",
    "Tich, a small mongrel, went through the North Africa and Italy campaigns with the King's Royal Rifle Corps, and won the Dickin Medal in 1949.",
    "Ricky, a Welsh Collie, cleared mines along a canal in the Netherlands in 1944, carrying on even after being injured by an explosion. He won the Dickin Medal in 1947.",
    "Peter, a Collie, found people buried under bombed buildings during the London Blitz. He won the Dickin Medal in 1945.",
    "Jet of Iada, an Alsatian, searched bomb sites during the Blitz and helped rescue many people. He was awarded the Dickin Medal in 1945.",
    "Mali, a Belgian Malinois, helped British special forces clear a building under fire in Afghanistan in 2012, despite being wounded. He won the Dickin Medal in 2018.",
    "Cairo, a Belgian Malinois, went with US Navy SEALs on the raid on Osama bin Laden's compound in Pakistan in 2011, guarding the outside of the building.",
    "Bob, a mongrel serving with the Queen's Own Royal West Kent Regiment, is said to have warned a patrol of hidden enemy soldiers in North Africa in 1943. He won the Dickin Medal in 1944.",
    "Sallie was the dog mascot of a Pennsylvania regiment in the American Civil War. A statue of her lies at the foot of the regiment's monument at Gettysburg.",
    "Satan, a French messenger dog in the First World War, is said to have run through heavy fire at Verdun wearing a gas mask, carrying a message that saved a group of trapped soldiers.",
    "Between 1943 and 1949, the Dickin Medal was awarded to 54 animals: 32 pigeons, 18 dogs, 3 horses and a ship's cat called Simon.",
    "Jack, an Airedale Terrier messenger dog in the First World War, is said to have carried a vital message through heavy shellfire in 1918, badly wounded, and died soon after delivering it.",
  ],
};
